var TOPICS = [{"n":1,"title":"Fire Dynamics & Fire Behavior","sub":"How fire actually behaves inside a structure — the vocabulary for everything else.","concepts":["Modern fires become <b>ventilation-limited</b> early: oxygen — not fuel — is the controlling factor.","<b>Vent-before-water feeds the fire;</b> water first cools and buys time. Air first buys intensity.","Your opening can <i>be</i> the ventilation event — coordinate water with entry and control doors.","Hose streams entrain air and move pressure: <b>water on the fire, not air into the room.</b>"],"videos":[{"title":"Fundamentals of Fire Science","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/124/fundamentals-of-fire-science","dur":"16 min","desc":"Fire science basics — the vocabulary everything else builds on.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain the fire tetrahedron and what removing each leg does to a fire","Describe the stages of fire development inside a compartment","Explain conduction, convection, and radiation heat transfer in plain terms"],"takeaways":["Fire needs fuel, heat, oxygen, and the chemical chain reaction — kill one leg and it goes out, but know WHICH leg you're actually breaking","Convection kills firefighters — superheated gases rise, bank down, and travel hallways ahead of the flame","Radiation preheats everything around the fire — that's what drives a room toward flashover before flame ever arrives","Modern synthetic fuel loads burn hotter and faster than legacy furnishings — treat every room fire as a fast one"],"drills":[{"q":"You're in a room and the ceiling is radiating intense heat down on you. Paint is blistering on the far wall but there's no flame in the room yet. What's happening?","a":"Radiant preheating — the fire next door is superheating this room's contents toward ignition. Cool the space or get water on the fire fast; you're one step from flashover.","why":"Radiation needs no contact — it preheats fuel to ignition temperature across a room. If you wait for visible flame, you're already too late."},{"q":"Fire in a closed bedroom, door shut. You open the door and the smoke layer ignites as fresh air hits it. What just happened?","a":"Backdraft conditions — the fire was ventilation-limited and smoldering. Your opening fed it oxygen and the accumulated gases ignited.","why":"A ventilation-limited fire doesn't go out, it waits. Opening without water ready gives it exactly what it was missing: air."},{"q":"Two identical rooms — one with legacy wood and cotton furnishings, one with modern synthetics. Which reaches flashover faster, and why?","a":"The modern one, by a wide margin. Synthetics have far higher heat release rates and produce hotter, more toxic smoke. Flashover in 3-4 minutes versus 20+ for legacy.","why":"Fuel load sets the clock. Modern homes are solid fuel wrapped in plastic — every room fire is a fast fire until proven otherwise."},{"q":"You're flowing water on a fire, it darkens down, then keeps reigniting. Which leg of the tetrahedron are you failing to break?","a":"Heat or fuel — surface cooling isn't reaching the seat. The fuel is still pyrolyzing. Get the stream to the seat of the fire, not the smoke.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on burning fuel breaks the chain. Aim at the seat, not the symptom."}],"fireground":"Every tactic you'll ever learn hangs on this: fire is chemistry and physics that doesn't care about your rank. Know the tetrahedron cold, respect how fast modern fuel loads go, and you'll understand WHY door control and water-first aren't opinions — they're physics."}},{"title":"Fire Dynamics in Structures","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/125/fire-dynamics-in-structures","dur":"17 min","desc":"How fire actually behaves inside structures; foundation for this whole topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain what 'ventilation-limited' means and why modern fires get there fast","Describe how any opening changes fire behavior inside a structure","Predict the flow path direction from vent locations"],"takeaways":["Modern structure fires become ventilation-limited in minutes — oxygen runs out before fuel does, and then the fire waits for air","Every opening you make is a ventilation event — doors, windows, and roof cuts all feed or redirect the fire","Fire moves from high pressure to low pressure — map the openings and you've mapped the fire's travel","The neutral plane tells you where the hot layer sits — watch it drop and you're watching conditions decay in real time"],"drills":[{"q":"Single-story ranch, fire in the rear bedroom, front door open, no other openings. Where's the flow path?","a":"From the front door (intake) through the house to the fire and out wherever it can exhaust. Anyone between the door and the fire is in the path.","why":"Air flows from high pressure to low pressure. The open door is the intake; the fire pulls everything toward itself. Your position relative to that path is a life-safety decision."},{"q":"You're on the nozzle approaching the fire room and the neutral plane — the smoke and heat layer boundary — is dropping fast toward the floor. What does that tell you, and what do you do?","a":"The fire is growing and the hot layer is descending — conditions are deteriorating toward untenable. Cool the space with the stream, stay low, and be ready to withdraw if it keeps dropping.","why":"A dropping neutral plane is the fire telling you it's winning. Water buys back the space; pride doesn't."},{"q":"Your captain wants a window taken on the fire floor 'to let the heat out' before water is flowing. What's your objection, backed by research?","a":"Venting before water feeds a ventilation-limited fire — heat release rate spikes and the fire races toward the new opening instead of being relieved by it.","why":"FSRI full-scale tests show vent-before-water intensifies the fire. Water first, then vent — in that order, every time."},{"q":"After knockdown you open up and find fire extension in the void spaces above the room of origin. What explains it?","a":"Convection drove superheated gases into the voids — heat rises and travels every concealed path it can find. Open up and check; the visible fire is never the whole fire.","why":"Fire doesn't respect drywall. Convected heat preheats voids, and extension is the rule, not the exception."}],"fireground":"Structures are boxes that contain and direct fire behavior — every door, window, and hallway is part of the system. Read the box before you fight the fire: where's air getting in, where's heat going, and where are YOU standing relative to both? The firefighter who reads the structure fights the fire; the one who doesn't just visits it."}},{"title":"Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/109/evidence-based-structural-firefighting","dur":"151 min","desc":"Full-scale burn experiments translated into modern fireground tactics — the capstone of this topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Summarize the key findings of FSRI's full-scale structural fire experiments","Explain why transitional attack improves interior conditions before entry","Apply experiment data to entry, ventilation, and water application decisions"],"takeaways":["Exterior water application before entry drops interior temperatures hundreds of degrees in seconds — the data is not ambiguous","Ventilation without coordinated water makes everything worse — the experiments measured it directly","Door control is the single cheapest, fastest tactic you own — a closed door is a 900-degree difference","Modern fires are ventilation-limited — which means YOU control the fire's air supply with every opening you make or keep shut"],"drills":[{"q":"First-due engine, working fire in a single-family home, crew ready to make entry. There's a charged line and a window with direct access to the fire room. What's the evidence-based play?","a":"Transitional attack — flow water through the window onto the fire first, then make entry. Interior temps collapse and the crew enters a survivable space instead of a flashover waiting to happen.","why":"FSRI's full-scale burns measured temperature drops of hundreds of degrees within seconds of exterior water application. Entering before water means fighting the fire at its worst for no reason."},{"q":"The truck crew is on the roof ready to cut while the engine is still stretching the line. What's wrong with this picture?","a":"Ventilation ahead of water — the roof opening will intensify the fire and pull it toward the opening, endangering interior crews. Hold the cut until water is flowing.","why":"Uncoordinated ventilation feeds a ventilation-limited fire. The experiments show fire growth accelerates the moment the vent opens without suppression."},{"q":"You're searching a bedroom with the door closed behind you. The hallway outside is fully involved. Why are you still alive?","a":"The closed door is holding back heat and smoke — compartmentation works. Keep it closed, search fast, and get out the window if the hallway doesn't improve.","why":"FSRI measured survivable conditions behind closed doors even with untenable conditions on the other side. Door control isn't theory — it's a wall between you and a thousand degrees."},{"q":"After seeing the research, your captain still says 'we've always gone in fast and vented early.' What's your professional response?","a":"Respect the rank, present the data — full-scale experiments, not opinions, show vent-before-water kills firefighters and transitional attack saves them. Ask to try it on the next one and compare.","why":"Tradition is just peer pressure from dead firefighters. The research exists so we stop learning from funerals."}],"fireground":"This course is the 'why' behind every tactic in this app. When you understand that exterior water resets the thermal environment, that doors are life-safety devices, and that ventilation is a weapon that points both ways — you stop doing firefighting by habit and start doing it by science. Watch the whole thing. Twice."}},{"title":"Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/021-flam-refrig","dur":"30 min","desc":"New A2L refrigerants change the fire and explosion risk picture — know what you're walking into.","lesson":{"objectives":["Identify A2L refrigerants and why they're replacing older types","Describe the fire and explosion hazards of flammable refrigerants","Apply safe tactics when refrigerants may be involved in a fire"],"takeaways":["Newer refrigerants (A2Ls) are mildly flammable — and they're in more homes and businesses every year","A refrigerant leak plus an ignition source in a confined space is an explosion hazard — treat unknown hissing near HVAC like a gas leak","These fires produce toxic byproducts — SCBA stays on through overhaul, no exceptions","Pre-plan it: know which occupancies in your district run large refrigerant charges — supermarkets, cold storage, ice rinks"],"drills":[{"q":"Supermarket fire — you hear hissing from the refrigeration racks and the air smells sweet and chemical. What's your move?","a":"Treat it as a flammable gas leak inside a fire environment — withdraw, isolate, SCBA on, no new ignition sources. Refrigerant lines may be compromised and feeding the fire or building an explosion hazard.","why":"A2L refrigerants burn. A damaged system in a fire is an uncontrolled fuel release — you wouldn't walk into a gas leak with a torch, so don't do it here."},{"q":"After knockdown in a commercial kitchen you want to take your mask off because 'it's just overhaul.' The building has modern HVAC with A2L refrigerant. Why stay masked?","a":"Damaged refrigerant lines can still be leaking, and burning or decomposing refrigerants produce toxic byproducts including hydrogen fluoride. Overhaul is when exposures happen.","why":"The fire's out but the chemistry isn't. SCBA through overhaul isn't paranoia — it's the lesson from every exposure study."},{"q":"Residential garage fire — homeowner says they were 'topping off the AC' with a DIY kit when it caught fire. What changes?","a":"Expect a compromised refrigerant circuit: possible ongoing flammable gas release, unusual fire behavior, toxic smoke. Attack from a safe position, stay masked, and don't assume it's a normal garage fire.","why":"DIY refrigerant handling means unknown quantities of flammable gas in an uncontrolled release. Size-up includes asking what was happening before the fire started."}],"fireground":"Refrigerants are the invisible fuel load nobody pre-plans for. You can't see the charge in a rooftop unit or a supermarket rack, but it's there — and the newer stuff burns. Add 'what's the refrigerant?' to your commercial size-up, keep your mask on through overhaul, and treat every hissing HVAC unit in a fire building like the hazard it is."}},{"title":"Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/90/firefighter-safety-and-photovoltaic-systems","dur":"30 min","desc":"Solar panels don't turn off when the sun's up — electrical hazards and tactics for PV fires.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why PV systems stay energized in daylight even with the main breaker off","Identify PV system components and their hazards on the fireground","Apply safe tactics for fires involving solar panels"],"takeaways":["Solar panels make DC power whenever light hits them — pulling the main breaker does NOT de-energize the array","Damaged panels and conduit are electrocution hazards — water and live DC are a bad combination","Don't cut through roofs with PV — know the setbacks and keep panels intact when you can","Arrays add dead load to roofs already failing in fire — weight and collapse are part of the size-up"],"drills":[{"q":"House fire with rooftop solar, daylight, you've killed the main breaker. Is the roof safe to work on?","a":"No — the panels and the DC conduit from array to inverter are still energized as long as there's light. Treat the whole array as live; avoid damaging panels or conduit.","why":"PV generates at the panel, not at the breaker. 'Power's off' is only true downstream of the inverter — the roof is still hot."},{"q":"The truck officer wants to cut a ventilation hole in a roof covered with solar panels. What do you tell them?","a":"Don't cut through the array — relocate the cut to a clear section or use another ventilation method. Cutting live panels in daylight risks electrocution, and damaged panels can't be turned off.","why":"A saw through a live DC conductor in daylight is an electrocution you ordered yourself. Ventilation isn't worth a dead firefighter — find another way."},{"q":"Flowing water on a fire in a room directly below a rooftop PV array — any electrical concern?","a":"Yes — water creates conduction paths from damaged array wiring, and runoff may be energized. Maintain distance, use fog patterns carefully, and remember the roof above you is an energized structure in fire conditions.","why":"Water conducts. A burning building with a live, damaged PV system overhead is two hazards stacked — treat it that way."},{"q":"Nighttime fire, same rooftop solar. Does the hazard go away?","a":"Reduced, not gone — moonlight produces minimal current, but battery storage systems are still fully energized and damaged wiring is still damaged. Don't assume safe.","why":"Batteries don't care what time it is. And 'probably dead' is not a safety procedure."}],"fireground":"Solar is on every third roof in the suburbs now — which means energized DC is part of your everyday structure fire. Build PV into your size-up: where's the array, where's the disconnect, where's the battery storage? The main breaker is not the off switch. Train like the roof is live, because in daylight, it is."}},{"title":"Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/111/the-science-of-fire-and-explosion-hazards-from-lithium-ion-batteries","dur":"50 min","desc":"Thermal runaway, vented gases, and explosion risk — the science behind battery fires.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain thermal runaway and how it propagates from cell to cell","Describe both the fire AND explosion hazards of battery failures","Apply safe tactics for battery-involved fires"],"takeaways":["Thermal runaway is a self-feeding chemical reaction — once one cell goes, it heats its neighbors until they go too","Vented battery gases are flammable AND toxic — white or gray wispy vapor before flame is your warning sign","These fires can explode — deflagration of accumulated vent gases in a confined space is a real killer","Water, lots of it, for a long time — and the battery can reignite hours or days later"],"drills":[{"q":"Garage fire — white vapor jetting from an EV battery pack, no flame yet. What's happening and what's your move?","a":"Cells venting flammable, toxic gases pre-ignition — the warning before thermal runaway fully develops or the vapor cloud ignites. Don't approach; flow water from distance if safe, SCBA on, and expect violent fire behavior or deflagration.","why":"That vapor is fuel looking for ignition. Walking into a vapor cloud from venting batteries is walking into a fuel-air bomb with a lit fuse nearby."},{"q":"You've knocked down an e-bike fire in an apartment. The battery pack is charred but intact. Can you clear the scene?","a":"No — isolate the battery, keep it under observation, and warn the occupant: thermal runaway can restart hours later. Soak it, separate it from combustibles, don't leave it inside the structure.","why":"Li-ion fires reignite. The call isn't over when the flame's out — it's over when the battery is cool, isolated, and watched."},{"q":"Basement fire with battery energy storage on the wall, heavy smoke, zero visibility. What's different about this entry?","a":"Everything — explosive vent gases in a confined space, extreme heat from thermal runaway, toxic fluorinated gases. Defensive or transitional attack until you know what you're dealing with; don't commit crews into a battery-involved basement blind.","why":"Confined space plus vented flammable gases plus ignition sources equals deflagration risk. Basements already kill firefighters; batteries make it worse."},{"q":"Your department has no SOP for battery fires yet. After this course, what's the one line you'd push for?","a":"'Treat every battery-involved fire as a hazmat incident with fire — full PPE and SCBA always, massive water, expect reignition, isolate and monitor.'","why":"Batteries are hazmat that burns. If your SOP treats them like a mattress fire, your SOP is going to get someone hurt."}],"fireground":"Batteries are in everything — bikes, cars, tools, walls, garages. They're a fuel load that makes its own oxygen, vents explosive gas, and reignites after you leave. The old rules (put the wet stuff on the red stuff, go home) don't cover it. Learn thermal runaway like you learned flashover, because you'll see it just as often."}}],"cards":[{"q":"You're first-due at 0200 on a single-family residential. All windows and doors intact, light smoke pushing from the eaves, no visible flame. Your captain orders a window taken on Side A for ventilation before the line is charged. Based on FSRI research, what happens to interior conditions in the next 60 seconds?","a":"They deteriorate fast — the fire is ventilation-limited (oxygen-starved), and the new opening feeds it: heat release rate spikes, temperatures climb rapidly, and the fire can race toward flashover.","why":"In modern fuel loads the fire becomes ventilation-limited early; oxygen is the controlling factor. Vent-before-water doesn't relieve the fire, it fuels it. Water first cools and buys time; air first buys intensity."},{"q":"Your crew advances down a hallway toward the fire room. The entry door is open behind you. What's the danger and what do you do?","a":"The open door behind you makes the hallway the flow path — you are standing between the intake and the fire, directly in the exhaust stream of superheated gases. Control that door (close it or post someone on door control) before advancing.","why":"Fire travels from high pressure to low pressure along the flow path. An uncontrolled door turns your advance route into the chimney."},{"q":"You're flowing water from the exterior through a window into the involved room (transitional attack) while the interior crew stages at the door. What does the research say happens inside, and what must the interior crew know?","a":"Interior temperatures drop hundreds of degrees in seconds and the heat release rate collapses — conditions improve dramatically. But it also generates a steam push, so the interior crew must be masked, ready to move in immediately, and aware the thermal picture just changed.","why":"Water absorbs enormous heat energy. Applied before entry it resets the thermal environment — but timing with entry is everything, and steam is the price."},{"q":"You're flowing a straight stream through a doorway into a ventilation-limited room. Besides putting water on the fire, what is your stream doing?","a":"It's entraining air and driving it ahead of the stream into the room — feeding oxygen to an oxygen-starved fire and altering the pressure/flow path.","why":"Hose streams move large volumes of air (air entrainment). In a ventilation-limited space, sloppy stream placement can intensify burning. Technique matters: put water on the fire, not air into the room."},{"q":"Thick black smoke, high heat at the door, no visible flame, windows intact. What stage is this fire in and what's your biggest concern?","a":"Ventilation-limited (under-ventilated), likely near flashover conditions. Biggest concern: your entry IS the ventilation event — opening that door can trigger rapid fire growth.","why":"Modern fires carry huge energy but little oxygen. Everything is loaded and waiting for air. Coordinate water with entry and control doors, or you become the trigger."},{"q":"You're in a room and the ceiling is radiating intense heat down on you. Paint is blistering on the far wall but there's no flame in the room yet. What's happening?","a":"Radiant preheating — the fire next door is superheating this room's contents toward ignition. Cool the space or get water on the fire fast; you're one step from flashover.","why":"Radiation needs no contact — it preheats fuel to ignition temperature across a room. If you wait for visible flame, you're already too late.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science"},{"q":"Fire in a closed bedroom, door shut. You open the door and the smoke layer ignites as fresh air hits it. What just happened?","a":"Backdraft conditions — the fire was ventilation-limited and smoldering. Your opening fed it oxygen and the accumulated gases ignited.","why":"A ventilation-limited fire doesn't go out, it waits. Opening without water ready gives it exactly what it was missing: air.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science"},{"q":"Two identical rooms — one with legacy wood and cotton furnishings, one with modern synthetics. Which reaches flashover faster, and why?","a":"The modern one, by a wide margin. Synthetics have far higher heat release rates and produce hotter, more toxic smoke. Flashover in 3-4 minutes versus 20+ for legacy.","why":"Fuel load sets the clock. Modern homes are solid fuel wrapped in plastic — every room fire is a fast fire until proven otherwise.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science"},{"q":"You're flowing water on a fire, it darkens down, then keeps reigniting. Which leg of the tetrahedron are you failing to break?","a":"Heat or fuel — surface cooling isn't reaching the seat. The fuel is still pyrolyzing. Get the stream to the seat of the fire, not the smoke.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on burning fuel breaks the chain. Aim at the seat, not the symptom.","src":"Video: Fundamentals of Fire Science"},{"q":"Single-story ranch, fire in the rear bedroom, front door open, no other openings. Where's the flow path?","a":"From the front door (intake) through the house to the fire and out wherever it can exhaust. Anyone between the door and the fire is in the path.","why":"Air flows from high pressure to low pressure. The open door is the intake; the fire pulls everything toward itself. Your position relative to that path is a life-safety decision.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures"},{"q":"You're on the nozzle approaching the fire room and the neutral plane — the smoke and heat layer boundary — is dropping fast toward the floor. What does that tell you, and what do you do?","a":"The fire is growing and the hot layer is descending — conditions are deteriorating toward untenable. Cool the space with the stream, stay low, and be ready to withdraw if it keeps dropping.","why":"A dropping neutral plane is the fire telling you it's winning. Water buys back the space; pride doesn't.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures"},{"q":"Your captain wants a window taken on the fire floor 'to let the heat out' before water is flowing. What's your objection, backed by research?","a":"Venting before water feeds a ventilation-limited fire — heat release rate spikes and the fire races toward the new opening instead of being relieved by it.","why":"FSRI full-scale tests show vent-before-water intensifies the fire. Water first, then vent — in that order, every time.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures"},{"q":"After knockdown you open up and find fire extension in the void spaces above the room of origin. What explains it?","a":"Convection drove superheated gases into the voids — heat rises and travels every concealed path it can find. Open up and check; the visible fire is never the whole fire.","why":"Fire doesn't respect drywall. Convected heat preheats voids, and extension is the rule, not the exception.","src":"Video: Fire Dynamics in Structures"},{"q":"First-due engine, working fire in a single-family home, crew ready to make entry. There's a charged line and a window with direct access to the fire room. What's the evidence-based play?","a":"Transitional attack — flow water through the window onto the fire first, then make entry. Interior temps collapse and the crew enters a survivable space instead of a flashover waiting to happen.","why":"FSRI's full-scale burns measured temperature drops of hundreds of degrees within seconds of exterior water application. Entering before water means fighting the fire at its worst for no reason.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting"},{"q":"The truck crew is on the roof ready to cut while the engine is still stretching the line. What's wrong with this picture?","a":"Ventilation ahead of water — the roof opening will intensify the fire and pull it toward the opening, endangering interior crews. Hold the cut until water is flowing.","why":"Uncoordinated ventilation feeds a ventilation-limited fire. The experiments show fire growth accelerates the moment the vent opens without suppression.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting"},{"q":"You're searching a bedroom with the door closed behind you. The hallway outside is fully involved. Why are you still alive?","a":"The closed door is holding back heat and smoke — compartmentation works. Keep it closed, search fast, and get out the window if the hallway doesn't improve.","why":"FSRI measured survivable conditions behind closed doors even with untenable conditions on the other side. Door control isn't theory — it's a wall between you and a thousand degrees.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting"},{"q":"After seeing the research, your captain still says 'we've always gone in fast and vented early.' What's your professional response?","a":"Respect the rank, present the data — full-scale experiments, not opinions, show vent-before-water kills firefighters and transitional attack saves them. Ask to try it on the next one and compare.","why":"Tradition is just peer pressure from dead firefighters. The research exists so we stop learning from funerals.","src":"Video: Evidence-Based Structural Firefighting"},{"q":"Supermarket fire — you hear hissing from the refrigeration racks and the air smells sweet and chemical. What's your move?","a":"Treat it as a flammable gas leak inside a fire environment — withdraw, isolate, SCBA on, no new ignition sources. Refrigerant lines may be compromised and feeding the fire or building an explosion hazard.","why":"A2L refrigerants burn. A damaged system in a fire is an uncontrolled fuel release — you wouldn't walk into a gas leak with a torch, so don't do it here.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants"},{"q":"After knockdown in a commercial kitchen you want to take your mask off because 'it's just overhaul.' The building has modern HVAC with A2L refrigerant. Why stay masked?","a":"Damaged refrigerant lines can still be leaking, and burning or decomposing refrigerants produce toxic byproducts including hydrogen fluoride. Overhaul is when exposures happen.","why":"The fire's out but the chemistry isn't. SCBA through overhaul isn't paranoia — it's the lesson from every exposure study.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants"},{"q":"Residential garage fire — homeowner says they were 'topping off the AC' with a DIY kit when it caught fire. What changes?","a":"Expect a compromised refrigerant circuit: possible ongoing flammable gas release, unusual fire behavior, toxic smoke. Attack from a safe position, stay masked, and don't assume it's a normal garage fire.","why":"DIY refrigerant handling means unknown quantities of flammable gas in an uncontrolled release. Size-up includes asking what was happening before the fire started.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Flammable Refrigerants"},{"q":"House fire with rooftop solar, daylight, you've killed the main breaker. Is the roof safe to work on?","a":"No — the panels and the DC conduit from array to inverter are still energized as long as there's light. Treat the whole array as live; avoid damaging panels or conduit.","why":"PV generates at the panel, not at the breaker. 'Power's off' is only true downstream of the inverter — the roof is still hot.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems"},{"q":"The truck officer wants to cut a ventilation hole in a roof covered with solar panels. What do you tell them?","a":"Don't cut through the array — relocate the cut to a clear section or use another ventilation method. Cutting live panels in daylight risks electrocution, and damaged panels can't be turned off.","why":"A saw through a live DC conductor in daylight is an electrocution you ordered yourself. Ventilation isn't worth a dead firefighter — find another way.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems"},{"q":"Flowing water on a fire in a room directly below a rooftop PV array — any electrical concern?","a":"Yes — water creates conduction paths from damaged array wiring, and runoff may be energized. Maintain distance, use fog patterns carefully, and remember the roof above you is an energized structure in fire conditions.","why":"Water conducts. A burning building with a live, damaged PV system overhead is two hazards stacked — treat it that way.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems"},{"q":"Nighttime fire, same rooftop solar. Does the hazard go away?","a":"Reduced, not gone — moonlight produces minimal current, but battery storage systems are still fully energized and damaged wiring is still damaged. Don't assume safe.","why":"Batteries don't care what time it is. And 'probably dead' is not a safety procedure.","src":"Video: Firefighter Safety and Photovoltaic Systems"},{"q":"Garage fire — white vapor jetting from an EV battery pack, no flame yet. What's happening and what's your move?","a":"Cells venting flammable, toxic gases pre-ignition — the warning before thermal runaway fully develops or the vapor cloud ignites. Don't approach; flow water from distance if safe, SCBA on, and expect violent fire behavior or deflagration.","why":"That vapor is fuel looking for ignition. Walking into a vapor cloud from venting batteries is walking into a fuel-air bomb with a lit fuse nearby.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries"},{"q":"You've knocked down an e-bike fire in an apartment. The battery pack is charred but intact. Can you clear the scene?","a":"No — isolate the battery, keep it under observation, and warn the occupant: thermal runaway can restart hours later. Soak it, separate it from combustibles, don't leave it inside the structure.","why":"Li-ion fires reignite. The call isn't over when the flame's out — it's over when the battery is cool, isolated, and watched.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries"},{"q":"Basement fire with battery energy storage on the wall, heavy smoke, zero visibility. What's different about this entry?","a":"Everything — explosive vent gases in a confined space, extreme heat from thermal runaway, toxic fluorinated gases. Defensive or transitional attack until you know what you're dealing with; don't commit crews into a battery-involved basement blind.","why":"Confined space plus vented flammable gases plus ignition sources equals deflagration risk. Basements already kill firefighters; batteries make it worse.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries"},{"q":"Your department has no SOP for battery fires yet. After this course, what's the one line you'd push for?","a":"'Treat every battery-involved fire as a hazmat incident with fire — full PPE and SCBA always, massive water, expect reignition, isolate and monitor.'","why":"Batteries are hazmat that burns. If your SOP treats them like a mattress fire, your SOP is going to get someone hurt.","src":"Video: Science of Fire and Explosion Hazards from Lithium-Ion Batteries"}],"diagrams":[{"title":"The neutral plane and flow arrows","cap":"Blue air flows <b>low</b> from intake to fire; red gases flow <b>high</b> from fire to exhaust.","svg":"t1d1"},{"title":"Ventilation-limited vs. seconds after ventilation","cap":"The same room: oxygen-starved fire, then violent growth the moment air arrives.","svg":"t1d2"},{"title":"Air entrainment","cap":"The stream drags air with it. Technique matters: water on the fire, not air into the room.","svg":"t1d3"}]},{"n":2,"title":"Flow Paths & Ventilation Tactics","sub":"Intake, exhaust, and the deadly ground between them — plus when (not) to vent.","concepts":["Flow runs from high pressure to low pressure: <b>intake → fire → exhaust</b>.","Every new opening redraws the map. Between intake and fire, you stand in the exhaust stream.","<b>Water on the fire first</b> (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex.","<b>Door control is the primary defense</b> — against fire growth and wind-driven blowtorch paths."],"videos":[{"title":"Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/162/understanding-flow-paths-low-intake-high-exhaust","dur":"5 min","desc":"Intake/exhaust and what's between them; the single clearest flow-path explainer.","lesson":{"objectives":["Define flow path — intake to exhaust — in your own words","Identify the flow path at a structure fire from its openings","Explain why firefighters must not position themselves in the flow path"],"takeaways":["Flow path is the route air takes from an opening (intake) to the fire and out an exhaust — fire, heat, and smoke all travel it","Openings CREATE flow paths — every door you open and every window you take redraws the map","Never be between the fire and the exhaust — that's standing in a blowtorch","Door control breaks the flow path — the simplest, most powerful tool you own"],"drills":[{"q":"Fire room at the end of the hall, front door open behind your crew, and the wind picks up, pushing into the front door. What just changed?","a":"The flow path intensified into a wind-driven fire — the open door is forcing air to the fire, increasing heat release and pushing fire toward the exhaust. Get the door controlled NOW.","why":"Wind turns a flow path into a blowtorch. The door is the throttle — close it and you choke the fire's air supply."},{"q":"Two openings: front door open, rear window failed, fire in the middle bedroom. Describe the flow path in one sentence.","a":"Intake at the front door, through the house to the fire, exhaust out the rear window — the hallway is the duct.","why":"Air in, fire, air out. Every interior space between intake and exhaust is in the path — plan your advance and your search around it, not through it."},{"q":"You're assigned door control at the entry point with the attack crew inside. What exactly are you doing, and why does it matter?","a":"Keeping the door as closed as possible — cracked only for the line — to limit air to the fire and keep the flow path from running through the crew. You're the fire's air supply valve.","why":"An uncontrolled door is an unlimited oxygen feed to a ventilation-limited fire. The person on the door controls fire growth with their hand."},{"q":"You're searching a room and feel a strong rush of hot air moving past you toward a window behind the fire. What are you in, and what's your move?","a":"You're in the exhaust leg of the flow path — between the fire and its vent. Get out of that path: move perpendicular to it, get low, or withdraw and re-approach from the intake side.","why":"The exhaust leg carries the fire's heat and gases at full force. Being in it means the fire is using you as its chimney."}],"fireground":"The flow path is invisible until it kills someone. Train yourself to see it: every opening is either an intake or an exhaust, and the fire lives between them. Control the doors, mind where you stand, and never let the building breathe easier than the fire already is."}},{"title":"Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/148/impact-of-horizontal-ventilation-on-fire-behavior","dur":"47 min","desc":"What ventilation actually does to fire behavior; the research behind water-first vs vent-first.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how horizontal ventilation through doors and windows changes fire behavior","Describe the research findings on ventilation timing relative to water application","Apply horizontal ventilation decisions to coordinated fire attack"],"takeaways":["Horizontal vents are the most common ventilation you'll do — and the most common way to accidentally feed the fire","Every window taken and door opened changes the fire's behavior — usually making it bigger before it gets better","The research is clear: horizontal ventilation before water increases heat release rate; after water, it clears the building","Control what you open, when, and why — uncoordinated openings just feed the fire room by room"],"drills":[{"q":"You're assigned to take windows on Side A 'for ventilation' while the line is still being stretched. What's your concern?","a":"Those windows become new intakes and exhausts that will intensify the fire before water is ready. Hold until the line is charged, or coordinate exact timing with the nozzle.","why":"Horizontal ventilation without water is the most common vent-before-water mistake. The research measured the heat release spike — it's real and it's fast."},{"q":"A fire-room window fails on its own from heat. What just happened to fire behavior?","a":"Unplanned horizontal ventilation — the fire got a new air supply and exhaust path. Expect intensification and growth toward the opening. Get water on it fast.","why":"The fire vents itself when it wants air. A failed window is the fire choosing its own ventilation — and it always chooses growth."},{"q":"After knockdown the building is full of smoke and heat. What's the horizontal ventilation play now?","a":"Systematic clearing — open windows and doors working with fans to move heat and smoke out, coordinated with the overhaul crew. Now ventilation does what people think it does.","why":"Post-suppression is when horizontal ventilation shines. The fire can't use the air anymore, so move it out for the crews still working."},{"q":"Wind is blowing hard into Side A where crews are making entry. What do you do about the openings?","a":"Control the windward openings — close or limit doors and windows on the wind side to prevent wind-driven conditions, and consider attacking from the leeward side.","why":"Wind plus openings equals blowtorch. Wind-driven fires kill firefighters; the openings are the throttle — manage them or the wind manages you."}],"fireground":"Horizontal ventilation is the everyday kind — doors and windows, every fire, every crew. That familiarity breeds carelessness, and it's the kind that feeds fires when mistimed. Every opening is a decision: make it deliberately, time it with water, and control what the wind does with it."}},{"title":"Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/103/coordination-of-suppression-and-ventilation-in-single-family-homes","dur":"140 min","desc":"Full-scale experiments proving why suppression and ventilation must be sequenced — the coordination bible.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why suppression and ventilation must be sequenced, not just simultaneous","Describe what FSRI's experiments measured when ventilation preceded suppression","Apply coordination principles to single-family fire attack"],"takeaways":["Water first, then vent — the experiments measured the difference and it isn't close","Ventilation before suppression increases heat release rate — you're feeding the fire you came to kill","Coordination isn't 'everybody go at once' — it's the RIGHT thing at the RIGHT time in the RIGHT order","The IC owns coordination — if suppression and ventilation aren't talking, they're working against each other"],"drills":[{"q":"Working fire, single-family, crews ready. Truck wants the roof, engine wants the front door. What's the coordinated plan?","a":"Engine gets water on the fire first — transitional or interior — THEN truck vents. Sequenced, communicated, confirmed. Ventilation follows suppression, not the other way around.","why":"The experiments are unambiguous: vent-before-water grows the fire; water-before-vent shrinks it. 'At the same time' without sequencing is just vent-before-water with extra steps."},{"q":"You're the IC. The interior crew reports untenable heat, but the roof isn't cut yet. Send them deeper or hold?","a":"Hold and get water on the fire first. Sending crews deeper into worsening conditions without suppression is how firefighters die. Cool it, then go.","why":"No tactic is worth a crew. Water resets conditions; courage doesn't. The coordinated attack starts with knocking the fire down, not with heroics."},{"q":"After knockdown, when DO you ventilate aggressively?","a":"After the fire's knocked and conditions are improving — then vent to clear heat and smoke for search and overhaul. Ventilation is a recovery tool once suppression has done its job.","why":"Ventilation has its place: after water. Clear the building for the search and overhaul crews, when the fire can't use the air you're giving it."},{"q":"A neighboring department's SOP says 'vertical ventilation upon arrival, all working fires.' Your take?","a":"It's outdated and dangerous per the research — automatic ventilation without coordinated suppression feeds ventilation-limited fires. SOPs should sequence water first.","why":"'We've always done it this way' isn't a tactic, it's a habit. The data says sequence it or lose."}],"fireground":"This is the most important coordination lesson in the fire service: suppression and ventilation are a team, but water bats first. Every fire you've ever been to had these two functions — the only question is whether they were coordinated or competing. Make them coordinated. Talk, sequence, confirm. The experiments don't lie."}},{"title":"Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/024-multi-family","dur":"115 min","desc":"The same coordination science applied to apartments — stairwells, shared spaces, and exposure units.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how multi-family fires threaten common spaces like stairwells and hallways","Describe coordination tactics that protect exposures and egress paths","Apply door control and suppression sequencing in apartment fires"],"takeaways":["In multi-family, the stairwell is the life-safety battleground — smoke there kills occupants above the fire","Opening the apartment door is a ventilation event that fills common spaces — coordinate water with entry","Exterior water before entry reduces the push of heat and smoke into hallways — same science, higher stakes","Protect the egress path like it's a victim — because it is one"],"drills":[{"q":"Apartment fire, 3rd floor, smoke showing in the common stairwell. Priority and tactic?","a":"Protect the stairwell — it's the egress for everyone above. Get water on the fire (transitional from exterior or balcony if possible) BEFORE opening the apartment door, control the door during entry, keep the stairwell door closed.","why":"An uncontrolled apartment door turns the stairwell into a chimney. Occupants above are counting on that stairwell — lose it and you have a mass-casualty event."},{"q":"You're forcing the apartment door. The line isn't charged yet. What's wrong?","a":"Everything — opening the door ventilates the fire into the common hallway with no suppression ready. Charge the line, then open, then flow water immediately.","why":"The door is the dam. Opening it without water pulls the plug — heat and smoke flood the common space instantly."},{"q":"Fire's knocked down but the stairwell is still charged with smoke and occupants are sheltering above. Now what?","a":"Ventilate the stairwell now that suppression is complete — roof hatch, fans — and communicate with occupants. Controlled, sequenced ventilation to clear egress.","why":"Post-suppression ventilation is exactly when it's supposed to happen. Clear the path, then bring people down."},{"q":"Garden-style apartment, fire in one unit, exposures both sides and above. Where does water go first?","a":"On the fire — always the fire first — with exposure lines protecting the adjacent units. Transitional attack knocks the main body while exposures are covered. Don't let one fire become three.","why":"Multi-family fires are exposure fires by design. Kill the main body fast and the exposures survive; chase exposures while the main body grows and you lose the building."}],"fireground":"Single-family tactics scale up, but the stakes multiply — you're not just fighting the fire, you're defending everyone else's home and their way out. The stairwell is sacred. The apartment door is a ventilation event. Water first, doors controlled, exposures covered. Apartment fires are won or lost on coordination."}},{"title":"Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/149/vertical-ventilation-and-suppression-tactics-in-residential-structures","dur":"49 min","desc":"When vertical ventilation helps, when it hurts, and what suppression-first changes.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain when vertical ventilation helps and when it harms","Describe how to coordinate roof ventilation with suppression","Identify roof hazards that change the ventilation decision"],"takeaways":["Vertical ventilation works when coordinated with water — and feeds the fire when it isn't","The roof is a hostile work environment: lightweight construction, fire below, limited escape","Cut where it counts — over or near the fire, with an escape route, with water ready","If the roof won't hold you, it doesn't get cut — no vent is worth a collapse"],"drills":[{"q":"You're on the roof: lightweight trusses, fire showing through the eaves below you. Cut or no cut?","a":"No cut — lightweight truss roofs fail fast and without warning, and fire at the eaves means the trusses are already compromised. Get off the roof; ventilate horizontally or go defensive on that section.","why":"Dead firefighters don't ventilate anything. A truss roof with fire impingement is a collapse waiting for a victim — don't volunteer."},{"q":"The hole is cut, water is flowing inside, conditions improving. What's the roof crew's job now?","a":"Get off the roof — the vent did its job. Lingering on a compromised roof after the tactical need is over is risk without reward.","why":"The roof is a means, not a destination. Cut it, confirm it's drawing, get down. Every extra minute up there is borrowed time."},{"q":"IC asks for vertical ventilation but the engine hasn't got water on the fire yet. Your response as truck officer?","a":"'We'll be ready to cut on your order, but we hold until water's flowing.' An uncoordinated roof hole feeds the fire and endangers the interior crew.","why":"A roof hole without suppression is a chimney for the fire to grow through. Coordinate or don't cut."},{"q":"When is vertical ventilation the RIGHT call?","a":"When water is on the fire or imminent, the roof is tenable, the cut is over or near the fire, and it will relieve conditions for interior crews and victims — sequenced, communicated, with an escape route.","why":"Vertical venting is a powerful tool in the coordinated attack — it just has to be part of the attack, not a solo act."}],"fireground":"The roof has killed more firefighters than almost any other assignment — and most of those deaths were preventable with coordination and roof-reading. Cut with purpose, cut with water ready, and know when the roof says no. Vertical ventilation is surgery: precise, timed, and never done alone."}},{"title":"Water Mapping in Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/80/water-mapping-in-residential-structures","dur":"18 min","desc":"Where water actually goes when you flow it — mapping stream reach inside real houses.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain what water mapping reveals about stream reach inside structures","Describe how to position for effective water application","Apply mapping insights to stream selection and nozzle position"],"takeaways":["Water doesn't go where you think it goes — mapping shows the real reach and distribution of your stream","Straight streams reach; fog patterns don't penetrate — match the pattern to the distance","Position matters more than pressure — a good position with moderate flow beats a bad position with big water","Know your stream's limits before you need them — the fireground is not the place to discover your reach"],"drills":[{"q":"You're at the front door flowing a fog pattern down a 40-foot hallway toward the fire room. What's actually happening?","a":"Very little water is reaching the fire — fog patterns lose reach and turn to steam in the hallway. Switch to straight stream to punch through to the seat.","why":"Fog is for close range and hydraulic ventilation, not for reaching down a hallway. Water that doesn't reach the fire is just steam in your face."},{"q":"From the front yard you can see the fire room window. Interior access is cut off. What's the play?","a":"Transitional attack — straight stream through the window onto the fire. Exterior streams reach the seat effectively with a direct line.","why":"The window is a gun port. If you can see the fire, you can hit the fire — and hitting it from outside resets conditions for everyone."},{"q":"You're flowing and the water seems to be 'disappearing' — no effect on conditions. Likely problem?","a":"You're not hitting the seat — the stream is striking a wall, the ceiling, or the thermal layer and vaporizing. Reposition for a direct line to the burning fuel.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on walls makes runoff. Only water on burning fuel puts out fire. Move until you're hitting it."}],"fireground":"Your stream is a tool with known capabilities — learn them before the fire. How far does your straight stream reach? Your fog? What changes from the doorway versus the yard? Water mapping turns guessing into knowing, and on the fireground, knowing is what puts the fire out."}},{"title":"Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/82/air-entrainment-by-fire-service-hose-streams","dur":"12 min","desc":"Your stream moves air as well as water — and that air feeds the fire.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how hose streams entrain — drag — air along with water","Describe how entrainment affects ventilation-limited fires","Apply stream management to avoid feeding the fire with air"],"takeaways":["Moving water drags air with it — every stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system","In a ventilation-limited fire, the air your stream drags in can intensify burning — the cooling usually wins, but know the tradeoff","Fog patterns move more air than straight streams — pattern selection has consequences beyond reach","Hydraulic ventilation uses entrainment on purpose — same physics, deliberate application, after the fire's knocked"],"drills":[{"q":"You're flowing a wide fog pattern into a ventilation-limited room and fire behavior intensifies momentarily. What happened?","a":"The stream entrained air into the oxygen-starved space — you fed it oxygen along with the water. The cooling will catch up, but expect that pulse. Switch to straight stream for penetration with less air movement if conditions allow.","why":"Your stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system. In a ventilation-limited fire, that air matters — it's fuel until the water overwhelms it."},{"q":"When is air entrainment your friend?","a":"Hydraulic ventilation — a fog pattern at a window deliberately pulling smoke and heat out of a room. Same physics, intentional application, after the fire's knocked.","why":"Entrainment isn't good or bad — it's physics. The firefighter who understands it uses it; the one who doesn't gets surprised by it."},{"q":"Straight stream versus fog for initial attack on a ventilation-limited room fire — which moves less air into the fire zone?","a":"Straight stream — it penetrates to the seat with less air stirred through the compartment. Fog patterns feed more air throughout the space.","why":"Pattern selection isn't just about reach — it's about what else you're putting into the room besides water. In an oxygen-starved fire, less extra air is better."}],"fireground":"Your nozzle does two things: delivers water and moves air. Most firefighters only think about the first. In today's ventilation-limited fires, the second one changes fire behavior — sometimes helping (hydraulic ventilation), sometimes hurting (feeding the fire). Know both, and choose your pattern like it matters. Because it does."}},{"title":"Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/88/suppression-tactics-in-single-family-structures","dur":"41 min","desc":"Transitional attack and exterior water application — knocking it down before you go in.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain transitional attack and when to use it","Describe exterior water application techniques","Apply suppression-first sequencing to single-family fires"],"takeaways":["Transitional attack means hitting it hard from outside, then going in to finish — it's not 'defensive,' it's smart","Exterior water doesn't push fire onto victims — the research measured it, and it doesn't","The first water on the fire matters more than the first firefighter through the door","'Aggressive interior attack' without water first isn't aggressive — it's just early"],"drills":[{"q":"First-due, working fire, single-family, clear shot through a window at the fire room. Interior crew is 2 minutes out. What do you do?","a":"Flow water through the window NOW — transitional attack. You'll knock the main body down before the interior crew masks up, and they'll enter a survivable space.","why":"Water on the fire beats firefighters near the fire. Two minutes of exterior water turns a flashover risk into a search-friendly environment."},{"q":"Your captain calls transitional attack 'not aggressive' and orders immediate interior attack with no exterior water. Your move?","a":"Follow the order — it's lawful — but know the research: immediate interior entry without water first means fighting the fire at its worst. Debrief after with the data.","why":"You follow orders on the fireground and fix tactics at the kitchen table. But know what the science says so the debrief means something."},{"q":"You've hit it from the exterior and conditions improved dramatically. Now what?","a":"Transition inside — advance the line, complete extinguishment, check for extension. Transitional attack is a phase, not a strategy: exterior knockdown, then interior finish.","why":"'Transitional' means you transition. The exterior hit buys the conditions; the interior crew closes the deal. Don't stay outside admiring your work."},{"q":"Does exterior water push fire, heat, or steam onto victims inside?","a":"No — FSRI measured this directly. Exterior water cools the space and does not drive fire toward victims; interior conditions improve. The 'push' myth is exactly that.","why":"This objection kept transitional attack sidelined for years, and the experiments killed it. Water from outside helps the inside — including any victims."}],"fireground":"Transitional attack is the biggest tactical shift of the last twenty years, backed by full-scale experiments, not tradition. Hit it from where you can, as soon as you can, then go in and finish it. The fire doesn't care where the water comes from — it only cares that it comes before you do."}}],"pfdNote":{"title":"PFD ventilation doctrine","src":"From the department's recruit Ventilation deck (Ch. 13)","items":["<b>Transitional attack.</b> Hit it from the outside <b>first</b>: an exterior offensive indirect handline into the compartment initiates cooling <i>before</i> transitioning to interior direct attack coordinated with ventilation — done prior to entry, search, or suppression. Prevents backdraft and flashover.","<b>The Three Ws before ventilating: When, Where, Why.</b> Timing rule: keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water — ventilation without water = fire growth. Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates.","<b>Never position between the fire and where it wants to go</b> without a hoseline or the ability to close a door — especially basement fires.","<b>Vertical ventilation alone</b> usually has no positive effect on a ventilation-limited fire; paired with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, it improves visibility and reduces temperature.","<b>Wind:</b> keep it at your back during attack; opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure O<sub>2</sub> into the fire.","<b>Flashover signs:</b> high heat, ghosting fingers of flame, sudden lowering of the smoke layer — do <b class=\"r\">NOT</b> enter; cool it transitional first, then vent, then enter.","<b>Hydraulic ventilation</b> (narrow fog/broken stream to push smoke/heat out): most useful for clearing a room <i>after</i> the fire is under control."]},"cards":[{"q":"Single-story home, fire in a rear bedroom. Front door is your entry; the rear sliding glass door is broken out. Where's the intake, where's the exhaust, and where do you NOT want to be?","a":"Intake at the front door (low), exhaust at the rear opening (high). The flow path runs front-to-rear straight through the house — do not position, search, or stage in that hallway without water on the fire and the door controlled.","why":"Flow runs intake → fire → exhaust. Everything between the intake and the fire sits in the exhaust stream of superheated gases heading for the exit."},{"q":"Truck company is heading to the roof for vertical ventilation on a single-family with fire in the attic. The engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. Per FSRI research, what's the risk and the correct sequence?","a":"Cutting the roof before water creates a powerful new exhaust that intensifies the fire below and can pull fire toward interior crews and openings. Correct sequence: water on the fire first (or truly simultaneous and coordinated), then vent.","why":"The vertical ventilation studies show vent-before-water increases heat release and worsens conditions for interior crews. Ventilation is a tactic, not a reflex — it must be coordinated with suppression."},{"q":"Basement fire, single-family, interior stairwell is the only access. What does the below-grade fire do to your flow path and your escape route?","a":"The stairwell becomes both intake and exhaust — superheated gases and smoke rise up the stairs into the first floor. Your entry path IS the exhaust path, and your escape route is compromised the moment you descend.","why":"Below-grade fires are stack-effect driven: the only opening becomes the chimney. Crews descending walk directly into the fire's exhaust. Consider exterior access/attack and control the basement door."},{"q":"You're second-due. First-due is making entry through the front door with a line. You see a neighbor has broken out a rear window \"to help.\" What do you do?","a":"Treat it as a tactical emergency: the flow path just changed. Immediately notify command and the interior crew, and either get that opening covered or adjust the plan — do not assume the original attack plan still holds.","why":"Every new opening redefines intake and exhaust. Uncoordinated ventilation can pull fire and lethal heat toward the new exhaust — possibly onto the crew inside. Ventilation must be coordinated, never random."},{"q":"25 mph wind at your back as you advance through the front door, fire in the rear of the house. What's the specific danger and the counter-tactic?","a":"Wind creates a forced blowtorch flow path from your entry (windward) to leeward openings — fire and 1000°+ gases driven down the hallway faster than crews can react. Counter: control the entry door, compartmentalize by closing interior doors, and never advance into a wind-driven exhaust path without water.","why":"Wind overrides natural buoyancy entirely. Door control is the primary defense — a closed door between you and a wind-driven fire is often the only thing that works."},{"q":"PFD doctrine: before taking a window or cutting a roof, what are the Three Ws and what's the timing rule?","a":"When, Where, Why — and keep openings closed until you're ready to apply water. Ventilation without water = fire growth.","why":"Water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water has slowed the fire, ventilation works for you instead of against you."},{"q":"You're assigned vertical ventilation on a ventilation-limited house fire, but the engine hasn't put water on the fire yet. What's the likely effect of cutting the roof right now — and what changes that?","a":"Little to no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. Pair the cut with coordinated exterior water as close to the fire as possible, and it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"PFD/UL-NIST research: venting alone on a ventilation-limited fire grows it. Water first resets the thermal environment; then ventilation helps instead of hurts."},{"q":"Fire room at the end of the hall, front door open behind your crew, and the wind picks up, pushing into the front door. What just changed?","a":"The flow path intensified into a wind-driven fire — the open door is forcing air to the fire, increasing heat release and pushing fire toward the exhaust. Get the door controlled NOW.","why":"Wind turns a flow path into a blowtorch. The door is the throttle — close it and you choke the fire's air supply.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust"},{"q":"Two openings: front door open, rear window failed, fire in the middle bedroom. Describe the flow path in one sentence.","a":"Intake at the front door, through the house to the fire, exhaust out the rear window — the hallway is the duct.","why":"Air in, fire, air out. Every interior space between intake and exhaust is in the path — plan your advance and your search around it, not through it.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust"},{"q":"You're assigned door control at the entry point with the attack crew inside. What exactly are you doing, and why does it matter?","a":"Keeping the door as closed as possible — cracked only for the line — to limit air to the fire and keep the flow path from running through the crew. You're the fire's air supply valve.","why":"An uncontrolled door is an unlimited oxygen feed to a ventilation-limited fire. The person on the door controls fire growth with their hand.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust"},{"q":"You're searching a room and feel a strong rush of hot air moving past you toward a window behind the fire. What are you in, and what's your move?","a":"You're in the exhaust leg of the flow path — between the fire and its vent. Get out of that path: move perpendicular to it, get low, or withdraw and re-approach from the intake side.","why":"The exhaust leg carries the fire's heat and gases at full force. Being in it means the fire is using you as its chimney.","src":"Video: Understanding Flow Paths: Low Intake, High Exhaust"},{"q":"You're assigned to take windows on Side A 'for ventilation' while the line is still being stretched. What's your concern?","a":"Those windows become new intakes and exhausts that will intensify the fire before water is ready. Hold until the line is charged, or coordinate exact timing with the nozzle.","why":"Horizontal ventilation without water is the most common vent-before-water mistake. The research measured the heat release spike — it's real and it's fast.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior"},{"q":"A fire-room window fails on its own from heat. What just happened to fire behavior?","a":"Unplanned horizontal ventilation — the fire got a new air supply and exhaust path. Expect intensification and growth toward the opening. Get water on it fast.","why":"The fire vents itself when it wants air. A failed window is the fire choosing its own ventilation — and it always chooses growth.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior"},{"q":"After knockdown the building is full of smoke and heat. What's the horizontal ventilation play now?","a":"Systematic clearing — open windows and doors working with fans to move heat and smoke out, coordinated with the overhaul crew. Now ventilation does what people think it does.","why":"Post-suppression is when horizontal ventilation shines. The fire can't use the air anymore, so move it out for the crews still working.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior"},{"q":"Wind is blowing hard into Side A where crews are making entry. What do you do about the openings?","a":"Control the windward openings — close or limit doors and windows on the wind side to prevent wind-driven conditions, and consider attacking from the leeward side.","why":"Wind plus openings equals blowtorch. Wind-driven fires kill firefighters; the openings are the throttle — manage them or the wind manages you.","src":"Video: Impact of Horizontal Ventilation on Fire Behavior"},{"q":"Working fire, single-family, crews ready. Truck wants the roof, engine wants the front door. What's the coordinated plan?","a":"Engine gets water on the fire first — transitional or interior — THEN truck vents. Sequenced, communicated, confirmed. Ventilation follows suppression, not the other way around.","why":"The experiments are unambiguous: vent-before-water grows the fire; water-before-vent shrinks it. 'At the same time' without sequencing is just vent-before-water with extra steps.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes"},{"q":"You're the IC. The interior crew reports untenable heat, but the roof isn't cut yet. Send them deeper or hold?","a":"Hold and get water on the fire first. Sending crews deeper into worsening conditions without suppression is how firefighters die. Cool it, then go.","why":"No tactic is worth a crew. Water resets conditions; courage doesn't. The coordinated attack starts with knocking the fire down, not with heroics.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes"},{"q":"After knockdown, when DO you ventilate aggressively?","a":"After the fire's knocked and conditions are improving — then vent to clear heat and smoke for search and overhaul. Ventilation is a recovery tool once suppression has done its job.","why":"Ventilation has its place: after water. Clear the building for the search and overhaul crews, when the fire can't use the air you're giving it.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes"},{"q":"A neighboring department's SOP says 'vertical ventilation upon arrival, all working fires.' Your take?","a":"It's outdated and dangerous per the research — automatic ventilation without coordinated suppression feeds ventilation-limited fires. SOPs should sequence water first.","why":"'We've always done it this way' isn't a tactic, it's a habit. The data says sequence it or lose.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Single-Family Homes"},{"q":"Apartment fire, 3rd floor, smoke showing in the common stairwell. Priority and tactic?","a":"Protect the stairwell — it's the egress for everyone above. Get water on the fire (transitional from exterior or balcony if possible) BEFORE opening the apartment door, control the door during entry, keep the stairwell door closed.","why":"An uncontrolled apartment door turns the stairwell into a chimney. Occupants above are counting on that stairwell — lose it and you have a mass-casualty event.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings"},{"q":"You're forcing the apartment door. The line isn't charged yet. What's wrong?","a":"Everything — opening the door ventilates the fire into the common hallway with no suppression ready. Charge the line, then open, then flow water immediately.","why":"The door is the dam. Opening it without water pulls the plug — heat and smoke flood the common space instantly.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings"},{"q":"Fire's knocked down but the stairwell is still charged with smoke and occupants are sheltering above. Now what?","a":"Ventilate the stairwell now that suppression is complete — roof hatch, fans — and communicate with occupants. Controlled, sequenced ventilation to clear egress.","why":"Post-suppression ventilation is exactly when it's supposed to happen. Clear the path, then bring people down.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings"},{"q":"Garden-style apartment, fire in one unit, exposures both sides and above. Where does water go first?","a":"On the fire — always the fire first — with exposure lines protecting the adjacent units. Transitional attack knocks the main body while exposures are covered. Don't let one fire become three.","why":"Multi-family fires are exposure fires by design. Kill the main body fast and the exposures survive; chase exposures while the main body grows and you lose the building.","src":"Video: Coordination of Suppression and Ventilation in Multi-Family Dwellings"},{"q":"You're on the roof: lightweight trusses, fire showing through the eaves below you. Cut or no cut?","a":"No cut — lightweight truss roofs fail fast and without warning, and fire at the eaves means the trusses are already compromised. Get off the roof; ventilate horizontally or go defensive on that section.","why":"Dead firefighters don't ventilate anything. A truss roof with fire impingement is a collapse waiting for a victim — don't volunteer.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures"},{"q":"The hole is cut, water is flowing inside, conditions improving. What's the roof crew's job now?","a":"Get off the roof — the vent did its job. Lingering on a compromised roof after the tactical need is over is risk without reward.","why":"The roof is a means, not a destination. Cut it, confirm it's drawing, get down. Every extra minute up there is borrowed time.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures"},{"q":"IC asks for vertical ventilation but the engine hasn't got water on the fire yet. Your response as truck officer?","a":"'We'll be ready to cut on your order, but we hold until water's flowing.' An uncoordinated roof hole feeds the fire and endangers the interior crew.","why":"A roof hole without suppression is a chimney for the fire to grow through. Coordinate or don't cut.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures"},{"q":"When is vertical ventilation the RIGHT call?","a":"When water is on the fire or imminent, the roof is tenable, the cut is over or near the fire, and it will relieve conditions for interior crews and victims — sequenced, communicated, with an escape route.","why":"Vertical venting is a powerful tool in the coordinated attack — it just has to be part of the attack, not a solo act.","src":"Video: Vertical Ventilation and Suppression Tactics in Residential Structures"},{"q":"You're at the front door flowing a fog pattern down a 40-foot hallway toward the fire room. What's actually happening?","a":"Very little water is reaching the fire — fog patterns lose reach and turn to steam in the hallway. Switch to straight stream to punch through to the seat.","why":"Fog is for close range and hydraulic ventilation, not for reaching down a hallway. Water that doesn't reach the fire is just steam in your face.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures"},{"q":"From the front yard you can see the fire room window. Interior access is cut off. What's the play?","a":"Transitional attack — straight stream through the window onto the fire. Exterior streams reach the seat effectively with a direct line.","why":"The window is a gun port. If you can see the fire, you can hit the fire — and hitting it from outside resets conditions for everyone.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures"},{"q":"You're flowing and the water seems to be 'disappearing' — no effect on conditions. Likely problem?","a":"You're not hitting the seat — the stream is striking a wall, the ceiling, or the thermal layer and vaporizing. Reposition for a direct line to the burning fuel.","why":"Water on smoke makes steam; water on walls makes runoff. Only water on burning fuel puts out fire. Move until you're hitting it.","src":"Video: Water Mapping in Residential Structures"},{"q":"You're flowing a wide fog pattern into a ventilation-limited room and fire behavior intensifies momentarily. What happened?","a":"The stream entrained air into the oxygen-starved space — you fed it oxygen along with the water. The cooling will catch up, but expect that pulse. Switch to straight stream for penetration with less air movement if conditions allow.","why":"Your stream is an air pump as well as a water delivery system. In a ventilation-limited fire, that air matters — it's fuel until the water overwhelms it.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams"},{"q":"When is air entrainment your friend?","a":"Hydraulic ventilation — a fog pattern at a window deliberately pulling smoke and heat out of a room. Same physics, intentional application, after the fire's knocked.","why":"Entrainment isn't good or bad — it's physics. The firefighter who understands it uses it; the one who doesn't gets surprised by it.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams"},{"q":"Straight stream versus fog for initial attack on a ventilation-limited room fire — which moves less air into the fire zone?","a":"Straight stream — it penetrates to the seat with less air stirred through the compartment. Fog patterns feed more air throughout the space.","why":"Pattern selection isn't just about reach — it's about what else you're putting into the room besides water. In an oxygen-starved fire, less extra air is better.","src":"Video: Air Entrainment by Fire Service Hose Streams"},{"q":"First-due, working fire, single-family, clear shot through a window at the fire room. Interior crew is 2 minutes out. What do you do?","a":"Flow water through the window NOW — transitional attack. You'll knock the main body down before the interior crew masks up, and they'll enter a survivable space.","why":"Water on the fire beats firefighters near the fire. Two minutes of exterior water turns a flashover risk into a search-friendly environment.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures"},{"q":"Your captain calls transitional attack 'not aggressive' and orders immediate interior attack with no exterior water. Your move?","a":"Follow the order — it's lawful — but know the research: immediate interior entry without water first means fighting the fire at its worst. Debrief after with the data.","why":"You follow orders on the fireground and fix tactics at the kitchen table. But know what the science says so the debrief means something.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures"},{"q":"You've hit it from the exterior and conditions improved dramatically. Now what?","a":"Transition inside — advance the line, complete extinguishment, check for extension. Transitional attack is a phase, not a strategy: exterior knockdown, then interior finish.","why":"'Transitional' means you transition. The exterior hit buys the conditions; the interior crew closes the deal. Don't stay outside admiring your work.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures"},{"q":"Does exterior water push fire, heat, or steam onto victims inside?","a":"No — FSRI measured this directly. Exterior water cools the space and does not drive fire toward victims; interior conditions improve. The 'push' myth is exactly that.","why":"This objection kept transitional attack sidelined for years, and the experiments killed it. Water from outside helps the inside — including any victims.","src":"Video: Suppression Tactics in Single-Family Structures"}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Floor-plan flow path","cap":"Blue intake arrows, red exhaust arrows — and the hallway between them, shaded as danger ground.","svg":"t2d1"},{"title":"Basement fire stairwell stack","cap":"Below grade, the stairwell becomes the chimney — your entry path is the fire's exhaust path.","svg":"t2d2"},{"title":"Wind-driven flow path","cap":"Wind turns your doorway into a blowtorch. Never advance into the exhaust.","svg":"t2d3"}]},{"n":3,"title":"Reading Smoke","sub":"Volume, velocity, density, color — the four things smoke is telling the first-due officer.","concepts":["Read <b>four</b> attributes: volume (extent), velocity (heat/pressure), density (fuel richness), color (fuel type and toxicity).","<b>Brown banking smoke</b> and pulsing/\"breathing\" doorways are pre-flashover warnings.","Yellowish, acrid smoke = synthetics and toxins (HCN). Full SCBA, zero skin exposure — search urgency goes <b>up</b>, not down.","Cleaner smoke after ventilation can mean a <b>bigger</b> fire, not a dying one. Read continuously, never once."],"videos":[{"title":"Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/98/impact-of-ventilation-on-fire-patterns","dur":"90 min","desc":"How ventilation changes what you see; the closest FSRI analog to dedicated reading-smoke training.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain how ventilation changes the fire patterns used in origin determination","Describe why pattern interpretation must account for ventilation effects","Apply ventilation-aware reading to both active fires and post-fire scenes"],"takeaways":["Fire patterns lie when you ignore ventilation — the deepest char isn't always the origin","Ventilation openings create patterns that mimic origin — clean burns near vents are about airflow, not ignition","The ventilation timeline matters as much as the pattern — when openings were made changes what the patterns mean","Read patterns with the full story: witness statements, suppression actions, and ventilation history together"],"drills":[{"q":"After a fire you find the deepest charring right next to a broken window. Is that the origin?","a":"Not necessarily — ventilation-fed burning near the window creates intense local patterns. The window fed the fire oxygen, so it burned hottest there regardless of where it started.","why":"Ventilation creates its own patterns. The hottest burn is often where the air was, not where the fire began."},{"q":"Two rooms, similar fuel — one has heavy clean-burn patterns, the other heavy soot. Likely difference?","a":"Ventilation. The clean-burn room had airflow feeding intense combustion; the sooty room was ventilation-limited. Same fire, different air, different patterns.","why":"Patterns record the fire's breathing, not just its location. Read the air supply behind every pattern."},{"q":"Why does the suppression and ventilation timeline matter for origin determination?","a":"Every opening firefighters made — doors forced, windows taken, roof cut — changed the patterns after the fact. Without the timeline you're reading a story with the chapters rearranged.","why":"Firefighters create ventilation that rewrites the patterns. The investigator who doesn't know what crews did is reading fiction."},{"q":"During overhaul you notice an unusual pattern that might indicate origin. What do you do?","a":"Stop, preserve it, notify the officer — don't pull it apart for overhaul until it's documented. Your overhaul can destroy the evidence.","why":"Every firefighter is a potential witness to origin. Destroying patterns during overhaul destroys evidence — pause and report."}],"fireground":"You'll read fire patterns twice: during the fire to find the seat, and after for cause. Both readings go wrong when you ignore ventilation. The fire you fight creates patterns with every opening you make — understand that, and you'll find the seat faster during the fire and protect the story after it."}},{"title":"Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/105/residential-fire-near-miss-incident-in-lagrange-ga","dur":"65 min","desc":"A real near-miss where crews misread conditions; practice spotting what they missed before the reveal.","lesson":{"objectives":["Reconstruct the LaGrange near-miss: what happened and why","Identify the misread conditions and the decision points","Apply the lessons to your own size-up and communication discipline"],"takeaways":["The crew misread conditions they could see — the information was there, the interpretation was wrong","Near-miss reviews are honest in a way incident reports aren't — use them","'We've done this a hundred times' is the setup for the one time it goes wrong","Every near-miss has a moment where someone could have spoken up — be that person"],"drills":[{"q":"The LaGrange crew pushed in based on a read that turned out wrong. What's your personal guardrail against the same mistake?","a":"Continuous re-assessment — conditions get re-read at every benchmark, and any firefighter can call a stop when what they see doesn't match the plan. The first read is a hypothesis, not a verdict.","why":"Fires change. The crew that locks in its first impression stops reading — and the fire keeps writing."},{"q":"In the review there's a point where conditions clearly deteriorated before the crew reacted. What should have happened at that moment?","a":"An immediate condition report and a tactical pause — 'conditions deteriorating, reassessing.' The delay between seeing and acting is where near-misses live.","why":"Recognition without action is just watching. The gap between 'that's getting worse' and 'we're backing out' is measured in injuries."},{"q":"Your crew watches this near-miss video. How do you make sure it changes behavior instead of just filling time?","a":"Debrief it against your own SOPs — 'where would OUR crew have made the same call?' Then drill the correction: condition reports, withdrawal criteria, mayday triggers.","why":"Videos don't change behavior; deliberate practice does. The near-miss is the case study — the drill is the lesson."},{"q":"A senior firefighter dismisses the review: 'That wouldn't happen to us.' Your response?","a":"'That's what they thought too.' Near-misses happen to experienced crews — that's the point. Complacency is the precondition, not the exception.","why":"Every crew in every near-miss review was experienced and confident. The lesson is for you, especially if you think it isn't."}],"fireground":"LaGrange is somebody else's worst day, given to you for free. The crew that misread conditions wasn't stupid or careless — they were normal firefighters having a normal fire until it wasn't. That's what makes it worth studying: it could be you. Watch it, debrief it honestly, and build the guardrails — continuous reads, spoken concerns, permission to stop — before you need them."}},{"title":"Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/106/garden-apartment-fire-near-miss-incident-in-cobb-county-ga","dur":"55 min","desc":"A real near-miss dissected — what the smoke was saying before conditions turned.","lesson":{"objectives":["Reconstruct how fire conditions developed in the Cobb County near-miss","Identify the warning signs the crew missed or misread","Apply near-miss lessons to your own size-up and communication"],"takeaways":["Near-misses are free lessons — someone already paid the tuition in risk, so take the education","The smoke was telling the story before the crew read it — conditions telegraph themselves to those watching","Multi-family fires punish small mistakes fast — common spaces turn against you with no warning","Speak up when conditions don't match the plan — the firefighter who says 'this doesn't look right' saves crews"],"drills":[{"q":"You're inside a garden apartment and the smoke conditions are worsening faster than the fire size seems to justify. What's your obligation?","a":"Call it out on the radio immediately — 'conditions deteriorating, smoke worsening beyond visible fire.' Don't wait for permission to report what you see.","why":"The near-miss turned on unreported conditions. The crew that speaks up early gives command time to adjust; the crew that waits gives command a mayday."},{"q":"The incident review shows the crew committed deeper while the smoke read 'ventilation-limited and growing.' What should the smoke have told them?","a":"That the fire was air-starved and any new opening — theirs or the fire's — would intensify it violently. The smoke said 'back out or get water first,' and they went deeper.","why":"Reading smoke isn't academic — it's the go/no-go gauge. When the smoke says the fire is waiting for air, you don't give it an opening without water."},{"q":"Your crew just watched this near-miss breakdown. What's the one change you make on your next multi-family fire?","a":"Continuous smoke reading with mandatory condition reports — every crew reports what the smoke is doing at every benchmark, and anyone can call a timeout when conditions don't match.","why":"Near-miss lessons only work if they change behavior. The crew that debriefs and adapts doesn't repeat the incident — the crew that just watches the video does."},{"q":"Command didn't have the full picture because interior reports were vague ('we've got some smoke'). What's the fix?","a":"Specific, standardized condition reports: smoke volume, velocity, density, color, and trend — 'moderate black smoke, increasing velocity, banking to four feet and dropping.' Vague reports produce vague decisions.","why":"Command can only decide on what they're told. 'Some smoke' tells them nothing; a real smoke reading tells them everything."}],"fireground":"Somebody almost died in Cobb County so you could learn this in a classroom instead of a burn unit. Honor that by actually changing how you operate: read the smoke continuously, report it specifically, and speak up the moment conditions stop matching the plan. Near-miss reports are the cheapest education in the fire service — but only if you use them."}}],"cards":[{"q":"From the front lawn: high-volume, high-velocity, turbulent BLACK smoke pushing hard from the eaves. Break down what volume, velocity, density, and color each tell you.","a":"Volume = extent of burning (a lot of fuel involved). Velocity = pressure/heat driving it (fast = intense heat). Density (thick) = fuel-rich, incomplete combustion. Black = hydrocarbon/petroleum-based fuels. Combined: a large, hot, ventilation-limited fire — expect violent change the moment it's ventilated.","why":"The four attributes each read a different variable — volume is extent, velocity is pressure and heat, density is fuel richness, color is fuel type and combustion efficiency. Read all four, not just one."},{"q":"Thin, wispy white-gray smoke drifting lazily from a soffit vent — versus thick BROWN smoke banking down inside the hallway. Contrast the two.","a":"The exterior smoke says early-stage or smoldering, lower heat. The brown banking smoke inside is the killer: brown means wood pyrolysis products at high temperature, and banking down to floor level means heat is filling the space from the ceiling down — a pre-flashover warning.","why":"Color tracks fuel and temperature; velocity tracks pressure; height tracks heat. Brown + low = the room is about to go."},{"q":"Smoke is pulsing — breathing in and out of the front doorway, inhaling then exhaling. What is this and what do you do?","a":"Textbook precursor to flashover or backdraft: the fire is gasping at the edge of its available oxygen. Do NOT enter through that door without water ready — cool the space first (transitional attack through a window if possible) and control the door.","why":"Pulsing means the fire is oxygen-limited and unstable. Your entry provides the air for explosive fire growth. This is the warning shot — heed it."},{"q":"Yellowish-gray smoke with a sharp acrid smell at a commercial occupancy. What does the color suggest and how does it change your urgency?","a":"Yellowish tint suggests nitrogenous/toxic products — synthetics, chemicals, or mixed commercial fuels producing hydrogen cyanide and other toxins. Full SCBA discipline, zero skin exposure, and search urgency goes UP: survivability in that atmosphere drops to minutes.","why":"Color hints at fuel chemistry, and chemistry determines toxicity. Reading smoke isn't just about fire behavior — it's a victim-survivability calculation."},{"q":"You vent a window and the outside smoke quickly goes from black and turbulent to lighter and faster-moving. Good sign?","a":"Not necessarily — it often means you've relieved pressure and the fire is now getting air and burning MORE completely and intensely. Cleaner-looking smoke after ventilation can mean a bigger fire, not a dying one. Verify with thermal conditions and keep applying water.","why":"Ventilation changes combustion efficiency. Smoke reading is continuous, not a snapshot — never declare victory off one change in appearance."},{"q":"After a fire you find the deepest charring right next to a broken window. Is that the origin?","a":"Not necessarily — ventilation-fed burning near the window creates intense local patterns. The window fed the fire oxygen, so it burned hottest there regardless of where it started.","why":"Ventilation creates its own patterns. The hottest burn is often where the air was, not where the fire began.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns"},{"q":"Two rooms, similar fuel — one has heavy clean-burn patterns, the other heavy soot. Likely difference?","a":"Ventilation. The clean-burn room had airflow feeding intense combustion; the sooty room was ventilation-limited. Same fire, different air, different patterns.","why":"Patterns record the fire's breathing, not just its location. Read the air supply behind every pattern.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns"},{"q":"Why does the suppression and ventilation timeline matter for origin determination?","a":"Every opening firefighters made — doors forced, windows taken, roof cut — changed the patterns after the fact. Without the timeline you're reading a story with the chapters rearranged.","why":"Firefighters create ventilation that rewrites the patterns. The investigator who doesn't know what crews did is reading fiction.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns"},{"q":"During overhaul you notice an unusual pattern that might indicate origin. What do you do?","a":"Stop, preserve it, notify the officer — don't pull it apart for overhaul until it's documented. Your overhaul can destroy the evidence.","why":"Every firefighter is a potential witness to origin. Destroying patterns during overhaul destroys evidence — pause and report.","src":"Video: Impact of Ventilation on Fire Patterns"},{"q":"The LaGrange crew pushed in based on a read that turned out wrong. What's your personal guardrail against the same mistake?","a":"Continuous re-assessment — conditions get re-read at every benchmark, and any firefighter can call a stop when what they see doesn't match the plan. The first read is a hypothesis, not a verdict.","why":"Fires change. The crew that locks in its first impression stops reading — and the fire keeps writing.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA"},{"q":"In the review there's a point where conditions clearly deteriorated before the crew reacted. What should have happened at that moment?","a":"An immediate condition report and a tactical pause — 'conditions deteriorating, reassessing.' The delay between seeing and acting is where near-misses live.","why":"Recognition without action is just watching. The gap between 'that's getting worse' and 'we're backing out' is measured in injuries.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA"},{"q":"Your crew watches this near-miss video. How do you make sure it changes behavior instead of just filling time?","a":"Debrief it against your own SOPs — 'where would OUR crew have made the same call?' Then drill the correction: condition reports, withdrawal criteria, mayday triggers.","why":"Videos don't change behavior; deliberate practice does. The near-miss is the case study — the drill is the lesson.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA"},{"q":"A senior firefighter dismisses the review: 'That wouldn't happen to us.' Your response?","a":"'That's what they thought too.' Near-misses happen to experienced crews — that's the point. Complacency is the precondition, not the exception.","why":"Every crew in every near-miss review was experienced and confident. The lesson is for you, especially if you think it isn't.","src":"Video: Residential Fire Near-Miss Incident in LaGrange, GA"},{"q":"You're inside a garden apartment and the smoke conditions are worsening faster than the fire size seems to justify. What's your obligation?","a":"Call it out on the radio immediately — 'conditions deteriorating, smoke worsening beyond visible fire.' Don't wait for permission to report what you see.","why":"The near-miss turned on unreported conditions. The crew that speaks up early gives command time to adjust; the crew that waits gives command a mayday.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)"},{"q":"The incident review shows the crew committed deeper while the smoke read 'ventilation-limited and growing.' What should the smoke have told them?","a":"That the fire was air-starved and any new opening — theirs or the fire's — would intensify it violently. The smoke said 'back out or get water first,' and they went deeper.","why":"Reading smoke isn't academic — it's the go/no-go gauge. When the smoke says the fire is waiting for air, you don't give it an opening without water.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)"},{"q":"Your crew just watched this near-miss breakdown. What's the one change you make on your next multi-family fire?","a":"Continuous smoke reading with mandatory condition reports — every crew reports what the smoke is doing at every benchmark, and anyone can call a timeout when conditions don't match.","why":"Near-miss lessons only work if they change behavior. The crew that debriefs and adapts doesn't repeat the incident — the crew that just watches the video does.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)"},{"q":"Command didn't have the full picture because interior reports were vague ('we've got some smoke'). What's the fix?","a":"Specific, standardized condition reports: smoke volume, velocity, density, color, and trend — 'moderate black smoke, increasing velocity, banking to four feet and dropping.' Vague reports produce vague decisions.","why":"Command can only decide on what they're told. 'Some smoke' tells them nothing; a real smoke reading tells them everything.","src":"Video: Garden Apartment Fire Near Miss (Cobb County, GA)"}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Smoke-reading reference chart","cap":"Four classics: early smoldering, large ventilation-limited, pre-flashover banking, pulse warning.","svg":"t3d1"},{"title":"Banked smoke and the thermal layer","cap":"Smoke banking down = heat filling from ceiling down. Stay below the layer.","svg":"t3d2"}]},{"n":4,"title":"Building Construction & Collapse","sub":"Construction type is your timeline — plus voids, cocklofts, and the roof that kills.","concepts":["<b>Construction type is your timeline.</b> Lightweight engineered trusses and I-joists can fail in 5–10 minutes under fire.","Type V = wood throughout; Type III = masonry exterior, wood interior. Read it off the walls.","<b>Cocklofts and concealed voids</b> spread fire unseen — pull ceilings and check voids early.","<b>Collapse indicators are lagging:</b> spongy floors, sagging roof, moving walls = withdraw immediately, collapse zone at 1.5× height."],"videos":[{"title":"Building Construction","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/133/building-construction","dur":"12 min","desc":"Construction types; the taxonomy for everything in this topic.","lesson":{"objectives":["Identify the five types of building construction and their fire performance","Explain how construction type changes your tactics","Predict collapse and extension risks from construction features"],"takeaways":["Construction type tells you how the building will fail — learn to read it from the curb","Wood burns, steel weakens, concrete spalls — every material has a failure mode and a timeline","Void spaces are fire highways — construction tells you where they'll be","Your tactics must match the building, not your habits — the building doesn't care how you usually do it"],"drills":[{"q":"You pull up on a Type V (wood frame) residential with fire through the roof. What's the construction telling you about time?","a":"Not much of it — wood frame fails fast under fire load, and fire through the roof means structural members are already compromised. Defensive or highly cautious offensive; no heroics in a collapsing box.","why":"Type V gives you the least time of any construction type. The building is telling you its limits — listen."},{"q":"Type III (ordinary) commercial building, fire in the cockloft. Where's the fire going and why?","a":"Laterally through the cockloft — the common void above the ceiling across the whole building. It'll run the length of the structure and drop down wherever it finds a path.","why":"Ordinary construction's cockloft is a horizontal chimney. If you don't cut it off, you'll chase fire through the whole block."},{"q":"Unprotected steel bar-joist roof, heavy fire below. What's the steel doing?","a":"Weakening and elongating — steel loses half its strength around 1,100°F and the joists push the walls outward as they expand. Collapse can be sudden and outward.","why":"Steel doesn't have to melt to fail — it just has to get hot. Bar-joist roofs under fire load are collapse hazards with a short fuse."},{"q":"Why does construction type belong in your first 30 seconds of size-up?","a":"Because it sets your tactics, your time budget, and your risk tolerance before you commit anyone. Wrong read on construction means wrong tactics in a building that's already failing.","why":"Size-up without construction is guessing. The building's type is the single biggest predictor of how the fire will behave and how the structure will fail."}],"fireground":"The building is your opponent's home field — it knows every void, every weak point, every collapse sequence, and it's not telling you. Construction knowledge is how you scout the field before the game. Read the building from the curb, predict its failures, and never ask firefighters to bet their lives against a structure that's already losing."}},{"title":"Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/76/structural-stability-of-engineered-lumber-in-fire-conditions","dur":"31 min","desc":"Engineered vs dimensional collapse timelines with the actual research data.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why lightweight trusses fail faster than dimensional lumber","Identify lightweight construction from the exterior","Apply appropriate risk limits for lightweight-involved fires"],"takeaways":["Lightweight trusses use less wood and metal gusset plates — they fail in minutes under fire, not tens of minutes","Gusset plates are the weak link — thin metal teeth that lose grip with heat, then the truss comes apart","You often can't see lightweight from the street — new-looking suburban homes are full of it","Fire in lightweight construction means rethink the risk — the building's clock is much shorter than you think"],"drills":[{"q":"Newer suburban home, fire in the attic. What are you assuming about the roof structure until proven otherwise?","a":"Lightweight trusses — assume rapid, unpredictable collapse potential. Limit roof operations, get water on the fire fast, and have an exit plan that doesn't depend on the roof holding.","why":"Modern suburbs are built with trusses. Assuming dimensional lumber in a 10-year-old house is wishful thinking that gets people killed."},{"q":"You're inside and hear a loud crack from above, then a section of ceiling drops. What's happening?","a":"Truss failure in progress — gusset plates letting go under heat. The roof/ceiling system is coming apart. Withdraw immediately along your planned exit route.","why":"That crack is the building's final warning. Truss failures cascade — one goes, the load shifts, and the rest follow. Don't wait for the second crack."},{"q":"How do you identify lightweight construction during size-up of an unfamiliar building?","a":"Age and style cues (newer residential = trusses until proven otherwise), wide clear spans with no interior bearing walls, pre-plan data, and asking — but default to assuming lightweight in modern construction.","why":"You can't see trusses from the curb, so you read the clues. When in doubt, assume the weaker system — the penalty for overestimating strength is death."},{"q":"The fire's in the lightweight floor trusses above the basement. What's the specific danger to crews on the first floor?","a":"Floor collapse into the basement — you're standing on a system that's burning beneath you, and truss floors fail without the sagging warning that dimensional lumber gives.","why":"Lightweight floors don't warn you — they just go. Crews above a basement fire in truss construction are standing on a trapdoor."}],"fireground":"Lightweight construction changed the deal: the buildings got weaker while the fires got hotter. Your grandfather's tactics assumed floors and roofs that would hold — yours can't. Learn to spot it, respect its timeline, and never trade a firefighter's life for a building that was designed to be cheap, not survivable."}},{"title":"Residential Attic and Exterior Fires","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/75/residential-attic-and-exterior-fires","dur":"42 min","desc":"Fires that start outside or in the attic — access problems and collapse risk in the overhead.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why attic and exterior-origin fires are tactically difficult","Describe access and water application challenges for attic fires","Apply safe tactics for fires starting on or reaching the exterior"],"takeaways":["Attic fires are hard to reach with water and easy to underestimate — the fire you can't see is doing the most damage","Exterior-origin fires (decks, eaves, siding) climb into attics and voids fast — chase the extension, not just the flame","Pulling ceilings to access an attic fire is a ventilation event — coordinate it with water ready","Roof integrity over an attic fire is always suspect — verify before committing anyone above it"],"drills":[{"q":"Deck fire extending to the eaves and into the attic. The visible flame is knocked down. Are you done?","a":"Not close — the attic and voids are the real fire now. Open up, check extension with the thermal camera, and chase it through the overhead until you're certain it's out.","why":"Exterior fires write checks the attic has to cash. The visible fire is the distraction; the hidden extension is what burns the house down an hour later."},{"q":"You need to pull ceiling to hit an attic fire. What's the coordination?","a":"Water ready before the ceiling comes down — pulling ceiling ventilates the attic and can intensify the fire. Nozzle charged, firefighter ready, then open up and flow immediately.","why":"Opening the ceiling is a ventilation event into a hot, fuel-rich space. Without water ready, you're just giving the attic fire the air it was waiting for."},{"q":"Attic fully involved, roof showing signs of compromise — sagging, spongy. Interior crew wants to push in for 'a quick knockdown.' Your call?","a":"No — defensive on the attic. A compromised roof over a fully involved attic is a collapse zone; hit it from outside (aerial, exterior streams) and protect exposures.","why":"There's no 'quick knockdown' under a failing roof. The attic already won that space — save the rest of the house and go home."},{"q":"Exterior fire on vinyl siding, running up the wall toward the eaves. What's the critical action in the first two minutes?","a":"Knock down the exterior fire fast AND get ahead of it inside — the eaves and attic are the next stop. Water on the wall buys time; a crew checking the attic prevents the real loss.","why":"Vinyl siding melts and the fire climbs. The exterior flame is the fuse; the attic is the bomb. Kill the fuse and defuse the bomb simultaneously."}],"fireground":"Attics and exteriors are where fires hide and where roofs fail. The fire you can see from the street is never the whole problem — it's the extension above and behind that decides whether the house survives. Open up early, coordinate your openings with water, and never trust a roof over a fire you can't see."}},{"title":"Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires","url":"https://training.fsri.org/course/83/understanding-and-fighting-basement-fires","dur":"41 min","desc":"The most dangerous residential fire — heat, limited access, and why basements kill firefighters.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain why basement fires are uniquely dangerous to firefighters","Describe the heat, access, and egress challenges of below-grade fires","Apply basement-specific tactics: exterior attack, stairwell protection, limited entry"],"takeaways":["Basements are heat traps — fire below you means the entire house becomes the chimney, and you're at the bottom of it","Access is limited and egress is worse — one stairwell in, same stairwell out, with fire between you and it","Basement fires have killed more firefighters than any other residential scenario — treat every one as high-risk","Exterior water into the basement before entry isn't optional — it's the difference between a fire and a funeral"],"drills":[{"q":"Fire in the basement, single interior stairwell, crew ready to descend. What's the play?","a":"Water into the basement from the exterior first — through windows, doors, or a basement access — to knock the main body down before anyone goes down the stairs. Then descend with a charged line, checking conditions the whole way.","why":"Descending into an uncontrolled basement fire means walking down into the heat trap with one way out. Exterior water first turns a death trap into a fire you can fight."},{"q":"You're at the top of the basement stairs and a wall of heat hits you — can't see the bottom, paint blistering on the stairwell walls. Go or no-go?","a":"No-go — that's an untenable stairwell and the basement is fully involved. Back out, hit it from the exterior, and reassess. The stairs are not an attack route right now; they're a chimney.","why":"The stairwell is telling you the basement's conditions. Firefighters who descend into that don't come back up under their own power."},{"q":"The basement has small windows at ground level on Side C. How do you use them?","a":"As attack points — break and flow water through them into the basement (transitional attack), and as ventilation coordinated with the attack. They're your best access to the seat without using the stairs.","why":"Basement windows are gun ports into the fire. Use them to put water on the seat from safety instead of carrying the fight down the stairs."},{"q":"Why do basement fires collapse floors above them, and what does that mean for the first-floor crew?","a":"The fire attacks the floor system from below — joists and supports burn unseen while crews work above. The first floor can drop into the basement without warning. Limit personnel above a basement fire and monitor the floor constantly.","why":"You're standing on the fire's ceiling. It doesn't sag politely like a roof — the floor you're on is being eaten from beneath. Respect it or fall through it."}],"fireground":"If there's one fire to fear, it's the basement. Limited access, brutal heat, one way in and out, and a floor above that's quietly failing. The research and the line-of-duty reports agree: hit it from outside first, protect the stairs, limit exposure above, and never let urgency override the physics of a heat trap. Basements don't give second chances."}}],"cards":[{"q":"Size-up: 2019-built single-family, fire in the attic, truss roof, lightweight engineered I-joists. What does the construction tell you about your timeline and tactics?","a":"Engineered lightweight components fail catastrophically fast — unprotected lightweight trusses and I-joists can collapse in as little as 5–10 minutes of fire exposure. Tactics: no prolonged interior ops above or below involved lightweight truss; go defensive earlier; establish a collapse zone.","why":"Engineered lumber has less mass and adhesives that fail at lower temperatures — FSRI research shows dramatically shorter collapse times than dimensional lumber. The building's age and construction type IS your timeline."},{"q":"Type V wood-frame vs Type III ordinary: you're looking at a 3-story apartment building. How do you tell them apart on size-up and why does it matter?","a":"Type V = wood frame throughout, everything combustible. Type III = masonry/noncombustible exterior walls with wood interior (joists, roof) — read it from the exterior: brick or block walls suggest III. It matters because fire spread and collapse differ: in III the walls stand while interior floors and roof burn and drop; void spaces, parapets, and concealed spaces drive the tactics.","why":"Construction type predicts both fire behavior and collapse mode. It drives the entire strategy — get it wrong and you're fighting the wrong building."},{"q":"Cockloft — define it, say where you find it, and explain why it kills firefighters.","a":"The concealed space between the top-floor ceiling and the roof deck, common in taxpayers, strip commercial, and older multi-family. It kills because fire runs undetected horizontally through it across the whole building, then drops down or causes sudden roof collapse on crews working below who never saw it coming.","why":"Concealed spaces defeat direct attack and hide fire spread. By the time a cockloft fire is visible, structural members are already compromised — hence early void inspection and pulling ceilings."},{"q":"Interior crew reports spongy floors. From outside you see the roofline sagging and a fresh crack in a load-bearing wall. Your call?","a":"Withdraw everyone immediately, switch to defensive, establish a collapse zone at 1.5x building height. Spongy floors = floor system failing; sagging roofline = truss/roof compromise; wall cracks = structural movement.","why":"Collapse indicators are lagging — by the time they're visible, failure is imminent, not approaching. The only safe action is evacuation. No property is worth a firefighter."},{"q":"Bowstring truss roof, commercial building, heavy fire showing. What's special about this roof and your tactics?","a":"Bowstring (arched) trusses are infamous for sudden, total, outward collapse with little warning — when one member fails the whole system unzips and pushes walls out. Tactics: no crews under a burning bowstring truss roof, defensive from the start if the truss is involved, big collapse zone.","why":"A historic firefighter killer with multiple LODD incidents behind it. Unlike other roofs it gives almost no sagging warning — the arch geometry fails all at once."},{"q":"You pull up on a Type V (wood frame) residential with fire through the roof. What's the construction telling you about time?","a":"Not much of it — wood frame fails fast under fire load, and fire through the roof means structural members are already compromised. Defensive or highly cautious offensive; no heroics in a collapsing box.","why":"Type V gives you the least time of any construction type. The building is telling you its limits — listen.","src":"Video: Building Construction"},{"q":"Type III (ordinary) commercial building, fire in the cockloft. Where's the fire going and why?","a":"Laterally through the cockloft — the common void above the ceiling across the whole building. It'll run the length of the structure and drop down wherever it finds a path.","why":"Ordinary construction's cockloft is a horizontal chimney. If you don't cut it off, you'll chase fire through the whole block.","src":"Video: Building Construction"},{"q":"Unprotected steel bar-joist roof, heavy fire below. What's the steel doing?","a":"Weakening and elongating — steel loses half its strength around 1,100°F and the joists push the walls outward as they expand. Collapse can be sudden and outward.","why":"Steel doesn't have to melt to fail — it just has to get hot. Bar-joist roofs under fire load are collapse hazards with a short fuse.","src":"Video: Building Construction"},{"q":"Why does construction type belong in your first 30 seconds of size-up?","a":"Because it sets your tactics, your time budget, and your risk tolerance before you commit anyone. Wrong read on construction means wrong tactics in a building that's already failing.","why":"Size-up without construction is guessing. The building's type is the single biggest predictor of how the fire will behave and how the structure will fail.","src":"Video: Building Construction"},{"q":"Newer suburban home, fire in the attic. What are you assuming about the roof structure until proven otherwise?","a":"Lightweight trusses — assume rapid, unpredictable collapse potential. Limit roof operations, get water on the fire fast, and have an exit plan that doesn't depend on the roof holding.","why":"Modern suburbs are built with trusses. Assuming dimensional lumber in a 10-year-old house is wishful thinking that gets people killed.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions"},{"q":"You're inside and hear a loud crack from above, then a section of ceiling drops. What's happening?","a":"Truss failure in progress — gusset plates letting go under heat. The roof/ceiling system is coming apart. Withdraw immediately along your planned exit route.","why":"That crack is the building's final warning. Truss failures cascade — one goes, the load shifts, and the rest follow. Don't wait for the second crack.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions"},{"q":"How do you identify lightweight construction during size-up of an unfamiliar building?","a":"Age and style cues (newer residential = trusses until proven otherwise), wide clear spans with no interior bearing walls, pre-plan data, and asking — but default to assuming lightweight in modern construction.","why":"You can't see trusses from the curb, so you read the clues. When in doubt, assume the weaker system — the penalty for overestimating strength is death.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions"},{"q":"The fire's in the lightweight floor trusses above the basement. What's the specific danger to crews on the first floor?","a":"Floor collapse into the basement — you're standing on a system that's burning beneath you, and truss floors fail without the sagging warning that dimensional lumber gives.","why":"Lightweight floors don't warn you — they just go. Crews above a basement fire in truss construction are standing on a trapdoor.","src":"Video: Structural Stability of Engineered Lumber in Fire Conditions"},{"q":"Deck fire extending to the eaves and into the attic. The visible flame is knocked down. Are you done?","a":"Not close — the attic and voids are the real fire now. Open up, check extension with the thermal camera, and chase it through the overhead until you're certain it's out.","why":"Exterior fires write checks the attic has to cash. The visible fire is the distraction; the hidden extension is what burns the house down an hour later.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires"},{"q":"You need to pull ceiling to hit an attic fire. What's the coordination?","a":"Water ready before the ceiling comes down — pulling ceiling ventilates the attic and can intensify the fire. Nozzle charged, firefighter ready, then open up and flow immediately.","why":"Opening the ceiling is a ventilation event into a hot, fuel-rich space. Without water ready, you're just giving the attic fire the air it was waiting for.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires"},{"q":"Attic fully involved, roof showing signs of compromise — sagging, spongy. Interior crew wants to push in for 'a quick knockdown.' Your call?","a":"No — defensive on the attic. A compromised roof over a fully involved attic is a collapse zone; hit it from outside (aerial, exterior streams) and protect exposures.","why":"There's no 'quick knockdown' under a failing roof. The attic already won that space — save the rest of the house and go home.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires"},{"q":"Exterior fire on vinyl siding, running up the wall toward the eaves. What's the critical action in the first two minutes?","a":"Knock down the exterior fire fast AND get ahead of it inside — the eaves and attic are the next stop. Water on the wall buys time; a crew checking the attic prevents the real loss.","why":"Vinyl siding melts and the fire climbs. The exterior flame is the fuse; the attic is the bomb. Kill the fuse and defuse the bomb simultaneously.","src":"Video: Residential Attic and Exterior Fires"},{"q":"Fire in the basement, single interior stairwell, crew ready to descend. What's the play?","a":"Water into the basement from the exterior first — through windows, doors, or a basement access — to knock the main body down before anyone goes down the stairs. Then descend with a charged line, checking conditions the whole way.","why":"Descending into an uncontrolled basement fire means walking down into the heat trap with one way out. Exterior water first turns a death trap into a fire you can fight.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires"},{"q":"You're at the top of the basement stairs and a wall of heat hits you — can't see the bottom, paint blistering on the stairwell walls. Go or no-go?","a":"No-go — that's an untenable stairwell and the basement is fully involved. Back out, hit it from the exterior, and reassess. The stairs are not an attack route right now; they're a chimney.","why":"The stairwell is telling you the basement's conditions. Firefighters who descend into that don't come back up under their own power.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires"},{"q":"The basement has small windows at ground level on Side C. How do you use them?","a":"As attack points — break and flow water through them into the basement (transitional attack), and as ventilation coordinated with the attack. They're your best access to the seat without using the stairs.","why":"Basement windows are gun ports into the fire. Use them to put water on the seat from safety instead of carrying the fight down the stairs.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires"},{"q":"Why do basement fires collapse floors above them, and what does that mean for the first-floor crew?","a":"The fire attacks the floor system from below — joists and supports burn unseen while crews work above. The first floor can drop into the basement without warning. Limit personnel above a basement fire and monitor the floor constantly.","why":"You're standing on the fire's ceiling. It doesn't sag politely like a roof — the floor you're on is being eaten from beneath. Respect it or fall through it.","src":"Video: Understanding and Fighting Basement Fires"}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Dimensional lumber vs. engineered components","cap":"Massive wood chars slowly; thin members and adhesives fail fast — collapse in minutes.","svg":"t4d1"},{"title":"The cockloft","cap":"Hidden horizontal highway for fire between ceiling and roof deck. Check voids early.","svg":"t4d2"},{"title":"Bowstring truss collapse","cap":"The arch unzips all at once and throws the walls outward. Big collapse zone, defensive early.","svg":"t4d3"}]},{"n":5,"title":"SCBA & Air Management","sub":"Buddy checks, the rule of thirds, low-air discipline, and the mayday math.","concepts":["<b>Low-air alarm = begin exit immediately.</b> Reserve is survival margin, not working time.","Rule of thirds: a third in, a third out, a third reserve. Turn before you burn the margin.","A downed firefighter gets your <b>Mayday (LUNAR)</b> — but never become the second victim.","<b>Looks clear ≠ is clear:</b> mask up during overhaul until the atmosphere is metered clean."],"videos":[{"title":"FSRI YouTube channel","url":"https://www.youtube.com/firesafetyresearchinstitute","dur":"channel","desc":"Search the channel for SCBA and air-management experiment footage. No SCBA-specific course exists in the FSRI plan, so the channel is the honest source.","lesson":{"objectives":["Use the FSRI YouTube channel to find air-management and SCBA-related experiment footage","Identify key air-management lessons from experiment videos","Apply air-consumption awareness to your own SCBA discipline"],"takeaways":["The channel is raw research footage — experiments, not lectures. Watch it with a student's eye, not a spectator's","Search it for SCBA, air management, and thermal exposure videos — the visuals teach what text can't","No single course covers SCBA here, so build your own curriculum from the footage — deliberately, not randomly","Every video is a chance to ask: 'what would my air gauge read right now?'"],"drills":[{"q":"You're watching an FSRI burn experiment — flashover at 4 minutes from ignition in a modern room. What does that tell you about air-supply planning?","a":"Your working time inside is bounded by fire growth, not just your bottle — the fire can outpace your air. Plan entries around fire-behavior timelines, not just gauge readings.","why":"The bottle gives you 30 to 45 minutes; the fire gives you far less. Air management means managing against both clocks."},{"q":"In experiment footage you see how fast conditions deteriorate once ventilation is introduced. What's the air-management implication?","a":"Deteriorating conditions raise your work rate — and your air consumption. Conditions worsen and the bottle drains faster exactly when you need more time to exit.","why":"Stress and exertion spike consumption. The moment you need air most is the moment you're burning it fastest — build the margin before you need it."},{"q":"The channel has no dedicated SCBA course. How do you still train air management from it?","a":"Watch experiments with your gauge in mind — time the evolutions, estimate your consumption at working rate, and practice checking air at every benchmark you see on screen.","why":"Air discipline is a habit, not a course. Every video is a rep if you use it that way."}],"fireground":"There's no SCBA-specific course in the FSRI catalog — this channel is the honest alternative: real experiment footage you study like game tape. Watch it actively: time the fire growth, track imaginary air consumption, ask what your gauge would read at each stage. The best air managers aren't born — they're built, one deliberate rep at a time."}}],"inHouseRef":"In-house reference: the department's own SCBA doctrine lives in the PFD Training library — <b>Recruit Training &gt; PowerPoints &gt; \"SCBA PP Presentation joe bonnell.pptx\"</b>.","pfdNote":{"src":"From the department's recruit SCBA deck — Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro","items":["<b>Cylinders.</b> 4500 psi, 45-minute, snap-change threaded fitting. Pressure lights: <b class=\"g\">2 green</b> = 76&ndash;100%, <b class=\"g\">1 green</b> = 51&ndash;75%, <b class=\"y\">1 yellow</b> = 36&ndash;50% (prepare to exit), <b class=\"r\">1 red</b> = 35% or less (leave immediately). Vibralert low-air alarm — audible <i>and</i> tactile — sounds at ~35%.","<b>PASS.</b> Pre-alarm after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that (or hit the red manual button).","<b>SOP (Vol 2).</b> SCBA SHALL be used in contaminated, may-become-contaminated, O2-deficient, or suspected atmospheres — explicitly including the active fire area, directly above it, gas leaks/fuel spills, visible products of combustion (even vehicle/dumpster fires), and untested confined spaces.","<b>Malfunction.</b> Red-tag it, report to the Captain, replace immediately; batteries changed only by a certified SCBA tech. Every member checks their SCBA at start of each shift and after each use."]},"cards":[{"q":"Your low-air alarm activates while you're searching a second-floor bedroom. Immediate action — and why is \"a few more minutes\" wrong?","a":"Begin exit IMMEDIATELY toward your entry point or hoseline; notify your partner and command. \"A few more minutes\" is wrong because on the Scott X3 Pro your Vibralert sounds at ~35% — that's roughly one-third of your cylinder left, and under exertion that's minutes of working air, not a comfort margin. Exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than you think.","why":"The alarm means you're on reserve, and reserve is survival margin, not working time. Most SCBA emergencies come from delayed exits, not empty cylinders."},{"q":"What's the rule of thumb for managing your air on entry, and why that ratio?","a":"The rule of thirds (or the department's air management policy): roughly a third of your air to work your way in, a third to get out, a third in reserve — check pressure at entry and turn when you've used your working portion.","why":"You must be able to exit under worst-case conditions — disoriented, low visibility, dragging a victim. The reserve isn't extra time; it's the margin that gets you home when everything goes wrong."},{"q":"Your partner's PASS is sounding and he's not answering the radio. Your own low-air alarm just activated. What takes priority and what's the procedure?","a":"The downed firefighter takes priority — call an immediate Mayday with LUNAR and his last known location so RIT deploys with full cylinders. But you cannot become the second victim: if you can't effect the rescue on your remaining air, withdraw and guide the rescue team in rather than dying next to him.","why":"A downed firefighter with no air is a rescue; a rescuer with no air is a recovery. Air discipline applies even during rescues — call it early."},{"q":"Overhaul. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off. Your call?","a":"Masks stay on until the atmosphere is metered and verified clear. \"Looks clear\" means nothing — overhaul atmospheres carry lethal CO and particulates with no visible smoke.","why":"Carbon monoxide and hydrogen cyanide persist at toxic levels long after visible smoke clears. Smoke exposure during overhaul is a leading contributor to firefighter cardiac events and long-term illness. Meter it or mask up."},{"q":"Buddy check before entry — what are you actually verifying on your partner's SCBA, and why does each item matter?","a":"Cylinder pressure (full — determines working time), PASS armed (no distress signal if he's downed), facepiece seal and regulator function (bad seal = smoke inhalation), HUD working (air awareness), radio on the right channel (can't call Mayday otherwise).","why":"Each is a failure point that kills. The two-minute check before entry prevents the fatal five minutes inside."},{"q":"You're watching an FSRI burn experiment — flashover at 4 minutes from ignition in a modern room. What does that tell you about air-supply planning?","a":"Your working time inside is bounded by fire growth, not just your bottle — the fire can outpace your air. Plan entries around fire-behavior timelines, not just gauge readings.","why":"The bottle gives you 30 to 45 minutes; the fire gives you far less. Air management means managing against both clocks.","src":"Video: FSRI YouTube channel"},{"q":"In experiment footage you see how fast conditions deteriorate once ventilation is introduced. What's the air-management implication?","a":"Deteriorating conditions raise your work rate — and your air consumption. Conditions worsen and the bottle drains faster exactly when you need more time to exit.","why":"Stress and exertion spike consumption. The moment you need air most is the moment you're burning it fastest — build the margin before you need it.","src":"Video: FSRI YouTube channel"},{"q":"The channel has no dedicated SCBA course. How do you still train air management from it?","a":"Watch experiments with your gauge in mind — time the evolutions, estimate your consumption at working rate, and practice checking air at every benchmark you see on screen.","why":"Air discipline is a habit, not a course. Every video is a rep if you use it that way.","src":"Video: FSRI YouTube channel"}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Buddy check","cap":"Verify before every entry: full cylinder, armed PASS, facepiece seal, working HUD, correct channel.","svg":"t5d1"},{"title":"Rule of thirds","cap":"A third in, a third out, a third reserve. The alarm means EXIT — not more working time.","svg":"t5d2"},{"title":"Overhaul atmosphere","cap":"Looks clear is not is clear. Overhaul produces toxic CO — mask up or meter it.","svg":"t5d3"}]},{"n":6,"title":"Medical Spanish — First Contact","sub":"High-value questions, instructions, and words you may hear during the first minutes of patient care.","concepts":["Start simple: <b>Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle.</b> — I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you.","Ask one short question at a time. <b>¿Qué pasó?</b> (What happened?) and <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> (Where does it hurt?) open the assessment.","Listen for urgent phrases: <b>no puedo respirar</b> (I can’t breathe), <b>dolor en el pecho</b> (chest pain), <b>me desmayé</b> (I fainted), and <b>estoy sangrando</b> (I’m bleeding).","Use these phrases to begin care, then bring in a qualified interpreter as soon as practical. This deck does not replace your medical protocols or a complete assessment."],"videos":[],"pfdNote":{"title":"Quick phrase card","src":"Plain-language Spanish for initial patient contact","items":["<b>¿Qué pasó?</b> — What happened? &nbsp; <b>¿Dónde le duele?</b> — Where does it hurt?","<b>¿Tiene dolor en el pecho?</b> — Do you have chest pain? &nbsp; <b>¿Le falta el aire?</b> — Are you short of breath?","<b>¿Cuándo empezó?</b> — When did it start? &nbsp; <b>¿Fue de repente?</b> — Was it sudden?","<b>¿Toma medicamentos?</b> — Do you take medications? &nbsp; <b>¿Tiene alergias?</b> — Do you have allergies?","<b>No se mueva.</b> — Don’t move. &nbsp; <b>Respire despacio.</b> — Breathe slowly. &nbsp; <b>Apriete mi mano.</b> — Squeeze my hand."]},"cards":[{"q":"You make first contact with a Spanish-speaking patient. Say: ‘I’m a firefighter. I’m here to help you. What happened?’","a":"Soy bombero. Estoy aquí para ayudarle. ¿Qué pasó?","why":"A short introduction establishes your role and intent, then opens the assessment without stacking several questions at once."},{"q":"A patient says, ‘Me duele el pecho y no puedo respirar.’ What are they telling you?","a":"‘My chest hurts and I can’t breathe.’ The key words are dolor/duele (pain/hurts), pecho (chest), and no puedo respirar (I can’t breathe).","why":"Those two complaints are high-priority cues. Recognizing the phrase should immediately focus your assessment while you follow protocol and arrange interpretation."},{"q":"Ask the patient where it hurts. Then name four answers you should recognize: head, chest, abdomen, and back.","a":"¿Dónde le duele? Listen for: la cabeza (head), el pecho (chest), el abdomen or el estómago (abdomen/stomach), and la espalda (back).","why":"The pattern ‘Me duele…’ means ‘My … hurts.’ Body-location words let you understand the first answer even before a full interpreter-assisted history."},{"q":"Ask: ‘When did it start? Was it sudden or little by little?’","a":"¿Cuándo empezó? ¿Fue de repente o poco a poco?","why":"Cuándo asks when; empezó means started; de repente means suddenly; poco a poco means gradually."},{"q":"You need medication and allergy history. Ask both questions in Spanish.","a":"¿Toma medicamentos? ¿Tiene alergias? If needed: ¿A qué? means ‘To what?’","why":"Medicamentos and alergias are close to their English equivalents, making them useful anchor words in a time-sensitive history."},{"q":"A trauma patient says, ‘Me caí, me golpeé la cabeza y me desmayé.’ Translate it.","a":"‘I fell, I hit my head, and I fainted/lost consciousness.’ Me caí = I fell; me golpeé la cabeza = I hit my head; me desmayé = I fainted.","why":"These are common phrases you may hear after a fall and each changes what you need to clarify during the trauma assessment."},{"q":"Give three simple neurologic-assessment instructions: ‘Smile. Raise both arms. Squeeze my hands.’","a":"Sonría. Levante los dos brazos. Apriete mis manos.","why":"Short, one-step commands are easier to understand and let you observe facial symmetry, arm drift, and grip while language help is arranged."},{"q":"Ask a patient: ‘Do you know where you are? Do you know what day it is?’","a":"¿Sabe dónde está? ¿Sabe qué día es hoy?","why":"These questions help test orientation. Sabe means ‘do you know,’ dónde means ‘where,’ and hoy means ‘today.’"},{"q":"Ask: ‘Are you pregnant? How many weeks? Is there bleeding?’","a":"¿Está embarazada? ¿Cuántas semanas? ¿Hay sangrado?","why":"Embarazada means pregnant; semanas means weeks; sangrado means bleeding. Ask clearly and privately when possible."},{"q":"You need the patient still and calm. Say: ‘Don’t move. Breathe slowly. I’m going to help you.’","a":"No se mueva. Respire despacio. Voy a ayudarle.","why":"These are respectful formal commands. Mueva means move, respire means breathe, and despacio means slowly."}],"diagrams":[{"title":"Body-location word map","cap":"Point and listen: cabeza, cuello, pecho, abdomen, espalda, brazo, and pierna.","svg":"t6d1"}]},{"n":7,"title":"Academy Skills","sub":"The core fireground skills drilled in the academy — SCBA, ventilation, search & rescue, fire behavior, ladders, and live fire — taught the way a PFD recruit learns them.","concepts":["<b>SCBA usage.</b> The Scott Air-Pak X3 Pro is your lifeline: 4500 psi, 45-minute cylinders, Vibralert at ~35%. Air discipline is non-negotiable.","<b>Ventilation.</b> The Three Ws — When, Where, Why — and the timing rule: no openings until water is ready.","<b>Search &amp; rescue.</b> Oriented, systematic search; VES with the door closed; the TIC extends your senses, never replaces them.","<b>Advanced fire behavior.</b> Modern fires go ventilation-limited early and punish air-first tactics. Read backdraft and flashover signs before they read you.","<b>Ladder operations.</b> 4:1 angle, heeled and locked, placed for the rescue window — never over the fire.","<b>Live fire scenarios.</b> The burn building is where the other five skills meet: coordinated attack, honest reads, and the discipline to back out."],"pfdNote":{"title":"Where this comes from","src":"City of Phoenix Legistar File #24-2678 — “Phoenix Fire Department Academy Overview”","items":["The city’s academy overview lists these same skills among the recruit fundamentals: SCBA usage, ventilation, search and rescue, fire behavior, ladder operations, and live fire.","These drills aren’t built from the city’s summary document — they’re built on the department doctrine already in this app: PFD SOP Vol 2 (SCBA) and the recruit Ventilation deck, Ch. 13.","Drill them until they’re automatic. The academy teaches the skill; the fireground tests it."]},"videos":[{"title":"Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/121/prerequisites-for-live-fire-training-participants","dur":"115 min","desc":"What you must know before your first live burn — safety, roles, and fire behavior in the burn building.","lesson":{"objectives":["Explain the safety roles and rules that govern live fire training","Describe how fire behaves in the burn building versus a real structure","Apply the prerequisite knowledge: PPE, SCBA, communications, and emergency procedures"],"takeaways":["Live fire training has killed and injured firefighters — the safety rules aren't bureaucracy, they're written in blood","Know your role before you go in: every position in the evolution has a job, and freelancing gets people hurt","The burn building is a controlled environment, not a real fire — the lessons transfer, the safety margins don't","If you don't know the emergency procedure, you don't go in — 'stop, mayday, withdraw' must be automatic"],"drills":[{"q":"During a live-fire evolution, conditions suddenly deteriorate — extreme heat, zero visibility, disorientation. What's your immediate action?","a":"Call it out, activate PASS if separated, and withdraw along the hose line or wall to the exit — exactly like the emergency procedure brief. Don't try to push through in training.","why":"The burn building is where you practice the withdrawal, not where you prove toughness. Firefighters who can't back out in training won't back out on the fireground."},{"q":"You're assigned as the backup team at the door during a burn. What's your actual job?","a":"Full PPE, on air, charged line, watching the entry point and the clock — ready to go in the moment the interior team calls for help or misses a check-in. Backup isn't standby; it's a rescue team pre-deployed.","why":"The backup team is the evolution's insurance policy. A backup team that's not ready is just spectators in turnout gear."},{"q":"The instructor asks if your PPE and SCBA passed inspection this morning. You skipped the check because it 'was fine yesterday.' What's wrong?","a":"Everything — live fire is the worst place to discover a failed regulator, a torn liner, or a low bottle. Pre-evolution checks are mandatory because the margin in a burn building is thin.","why":"Gear failures in training are embarrassing; gear failures in a live burn are injuries. The check takes two minutes. The burn takes seconds."},{"q":"Why does the course stress that burn building fires behave differently than real structure fires?","a":"Because the fuel loads are controlled, the ventilation is designed, and instructors are managing the evolution — it's safer by design, which means the lessons about reading conditions still apply but the consequences are managed. Don't mistake training safety for fireground safety.","why":"Overconfidence from clean burns kills on real fires. The burn building teaches skills; only real fires teach respect. Keep them separate in your head."}],"fireground":"Live fire training is the closest thing to the real thing — and that's exactly why the prerequisites matter. The firefighters who get hurt in burns are the ones who treated it casually: skipped checks, freelanced roles, ignored the safety brief. Be the booter who takes training more seriously than anyone else there. The habits you build in the burn building are the ones you'll have at 0200 when it counts."}},{"title":"Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","url":"http://training.fsri.org/course/171/search-and-rescue-tactics-in-single-family-single-story-residential-structures","dur":"115 min","desc":"FSRI search experiments — where victims are found and how search timing beats fire growth.","lesson":{"objectives":["Summarize what FSRI's search experiments found about victim locations and survivability","Explain how search timing interacts with fire growth and suppression","Apply systematic search techniques for single-story residential structures"],"takeaways":["Victims are found in predictable places — bedrooms, near exits, behind closed doors — search there first, not everywhere equally","Speed matters more than perfection — a fast systematic search beats a slow thorough one when the fire's growing","Closed doors save victims too — a victim behind a closed door buys you time; check closed rooms early","Search is coordinated with suppression — water on the fire is what makes the search survivable, for victims and searchers"],"drills":[{"q":"Single-story home, fire in the living room, possible victim — where do you search first?","a":"Bedrooms and the paths to exits — that's where people are when fire strikes, especially at night. Sweep the bedrooms first, then bathrooms and closets, working back toward the fire with the line protecting you.","why":"The experiments show victims cluster in predictable locations. Searching randomly wastes the survivability window; searching smart buys lives."},{"q":"You're searching and find a closed bedroom door with no fire showing. Open it or skip it?","a":"Open it — carefully, staying low, ready to close it again. Closed doors protect victims; that room is the most likely survivable space in the house. Check it.","why":"A closed door is a victim's best friend. The experiments confirm survivable conditions persist behind closed doors long after the hallway becomes untenable. Never skip a closed door."},{"q":"The attack crew is hitting the fire from the exterior (transitional) while you prepare to search. How does that change your search?","a":"It makes it survivable — the exterior knockdown drops temperatures and buys time. Coordinate: confirm water's on the fire, then search fast and systematically while conditions are improved.","why":"Search without suppression is a race against flashover; search after transitional attack is a rescue in a cooled building. The nozzle is the search team's best protection."},{"q":"You've searched the bedrooms and found no one, but dispatch said 'child possibly inside.' What's next?","a":"Re-search — expand to bathrooms, closets, under beds, behind furniture, and re-check rooms as conditions change. Kids hide. And communicate: tell command the primary search is complete with negative results so they can decide on a secondary.","why":"Children don't wait by the door — they hide from the scary thing. A negative primary search isn't clearance; it's the start of the secondary. Report it clearly so command has real information."}],"fireground":"Search is why we're here — everything else is in service of getting people out alive. The research gives you an edge: know where victims are likely to be, move fast and systematically, coordinate with the line, and respect the closed door. A good searcher is part scientist, part athlete, and all heart. Train the system until it's automatic, because in the smoke and heat, automatic is all you have."}}],"cards":[{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Academy burn-building evolution. Your HUD shows one yellow pressure light — 36–50% — and your partner says there’s plenty of air left to finish the search. Your call?","a":"He’s wrong — you start planning your exit now. On the X3 Pro, 1 yellow means 36–50% remaining, and under real exertion that disappears fast. The next step down is the red light at 35% with the Vibralert screaming, and that means LEAVE — not ‘a few more minutes.’","why":"The HUD is a countdown, not a gauge: 2 green is 76–100%, 1 green 51–75%, 1 yellow 36–50% (prepare to exit), 1 red 35% or less (leave immediately). Recruits consistently overestimate what half a cylinder buys under work rate — respect the yellow."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Interior search: your low-air alarm activates — audible AND tactile Vibralert. Your partner is mid-bedroom and says ‘almost done.’ What’s the move, and why does the double signal matter?","a":"Exit begins immediately — both of you head for your entry point or hoseline and notify command. The double signal exists because one sense can fail you: in a roaring IDLH you might not hear the beep, but you will feel the shake. It fires at ~35%, which is reserve air — survival margin, not working time.","why":"Same discipline as Topic 5: the alarm means you’re on the last third of your air. ‘Almost done’ has killed firefighters — exiting through deteriorating conditions always takes longer than the search you’re finishing."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"You go motionless about 25 seconds while your partner checks a closet. Your PASS gives its pre-alarm chirp, and he snaps that you’ll set it off if you keep stopping. What do you tell him?","a":"Good — that’s exactly what it’s supposed to do. Pre-alarm sounds after 20 seconds motionless, full alarm 12 seconds after that, and there’s a manual red button for when you need it now. A PASS that nags you is a PASS that’s working; the one time it matters, you want it loud.","why":"PASS discipline: armed before entry, respected during ops. Firefighters who silence or ignore motion alarms remove the one device that calls for them when they can’t call for themselves."},{"sec":"SCBA usage","q":"Knockdown is done on a dumpster fire. The smoke has mostly cleared and the crew wants masks off to pull the pile apart. PFD SOP Vol 2 says what?","a":"Masks stay on. SOP requires SCBA in atmospheres that are contaminated or may become contaminated — and it names visible products of combustion explicitly, including vehicle and dumpster fires, plus gas leaks, fuel spills, the active fire area and directly above it. Overhaul on a smoldering pile is a contamination event waiting to happen.","why":"Looks clear is not is clear, and SOP Vol 2 doesn’t leave it to judgment — the listed atmospheres get SCBA, period. Dumpster and vehicle fires are called out by name because crews habitually underrate them."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Academy evolution: your crew is assigned to take windows on Side A while the engine is still stretching the line. The instructor asks for the Three Ws first. What are they, and what timing rule do they enforce?","a":"When, Where, Why — and the rule is: keep openings closed until you’re ready to apply water. Ventilation without water equals fire growth. The Ws force you to justify the opening against a water plan instead of venting on reflex.","why":"Straight from the recruit Vent Ch. 13: water absorbs more heat energy than the fire creates. Once water is applied, ventilation works for you; before that, it works for the fire. Most recruits vent because it feels like doing something — the Three Ws make it a tactic."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Burn building, ventilation-limited room fire. The instructor has you hit it with an exterior offensive indirect handline through the window FIRST, before anyone goes inside. What is this called, and which two killers does it prevent?","a":"Transitional attack — exterior cooling before transitioning to interior direct attack, coordinated with ventilation, done prior to entry, search, or suppression. It prevents backdraft and flashover by collapsing the heat release rate before crews commit inside.","why":"PFD recruit ventilation doctrine: interior temperatures can drop hundreds of degrees in seconds. Your entry is the ventilation event — transitional attack makes sure the fire is already losing when you open the door, instead of waiting for you to feed it."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"Live burn, 20 mph wind hitting the same side as your entry door. The instructor asks: where do you want the wind, and what happens if you open the upwind side?","a":"Keep the wind at your back during attack. Opening the upwind side feeds unlimited high-pressure oxygen into the fire and turns the structure into a blowtorch aimed downwind — straight at anyone inside or exiting on the lee side.","why":"PFD doctrine. Wind doesn’t nudge the flow path, it owns it — it overrides natural buoyancy entirely. Windward openings are fuel; door control and wind-aware positioning are the only defenses."},{"sec":"Ventilation","q":"A classmate says: ‘The room is oxygen-starved, so let’s cut the roof — ventilation can only help.’ Using recruit vent doctrine, why is he wrong, and when does the cut actually help?","a":"He’s wrong because on a ventilation-limited fire, vertical ventilation alone usually has no positive effect — the new exhaust just feeds the oxygen-starved fire. The cut helps when paired with coordinated exterior water applied as close to the fire as possible: then it improves visibility and drops temperatures.","why":"The research the department teaches: venting alone grows a ventilation-limited fire. The roof cut is a tool for a cooled fire, not a substitute for cooling. Sequence is the doctrine."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Burn-building search, zero visibility. Your partner loses the wall and starts free-searching the middle of the room ‘to cover more ground.’ What’s wrong, and what’s the fix?","a":"He’s unoriented — free-searching the middle of a room is how firefighters die in buildings they can’t see. Fix: re-establish contact with the wall or the hoseline, re-orient to your entry point, and search systematically off a known reference. Coverage means nothing without orientation.","why":"Academy search doctrine: stay oriented to something that leads out — wall, line, rope. An unoriented searcher isn’t searching, they’re wandering, and a wanderer becomes the second victim RIT has to find."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"You’re assigned VES on a second-floor bedroom window in a live burn: vent, enter, search. Your partner wants to leave the bedroom door open ‘for a second way out.’ Correct him.","a":"Wrong — the door stays closed. VES works because the closed door isolates the room from the fire and the flow path; you vent the window, enter, and search from a tenable space. Opening the door invites the fire and its exhaust straight into your search area — and turns your window into the exhaust.","why":"VES is a disciplined, isolated tactic: window in, door closed, search, window out. The second way out is the window you came through. An open door doesn’t add an exit, it adds a flow path."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Your crew has a TIC on the search. The camera shows a clear room and your officer waves you ahead faster. What’s the right use of the camera — and what mistake is the officer making?","a":"The TIC is a scanning tool, not a substitute for contact, orientation, and disciplined technique. Use it to scan and prioritize, but stay low, keep your reference, and search to the standard. The mistake: outrunning your orientation because the picture looks clean — cameras miss victims, don’t show floor integrity, and die at the worst moment.","why":"The academy lesson: the TIC extends your senses, it doesn’t replace your fundamentals. Every generation finds a new technology to trust more than its training. Don’t be that evolution."},{"sec":"Search & rescue","q":"Second-floor search, conditions deteriorating fast, and you realize you don’t know which way the stairs are. Immediate action — and what should have prevented this?","a":"Stop, call it out, and declare a Mayday with LUNAR early — while you still have air and composure — so RIT comes with full cylinders to your last known location. What should have prevented it: continuous orientation — counting doors, wall and line contact, and a pre-entry plan with an exit reference.","why":"Lost firefighters die from delayed Maydays, not from being lost. Call it while you can still talk and breathe. And the prevention is boring on purpose: orientation is a habit, not a skill you summon once you’re already lost."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"Academy classroom: why do modern house fires reach flashover faster than the legacy fires in the old textbooks? Give the two-part answer.","a":"One: modern fuel loads — synthetics, open floor plans, lightweight construction — release heat far faster than legacy natural-fiber furnishings. Two: tighter, energy-efficient homes make fires ventilation-limited early, so the moment air arrives — your entry, a window failing — the stored energy releases violently. Faster to flashover AND more explosive when ventilated.","why":"This is the ‘advanced’ in advanced fire behavior: not just hotter fires, but a different fire. Legacy tactics assumed fuel-limited fires with time; modern fires are oxygen-limited with a hair trigger. Everything else in the academy skill set assumes you understand this."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"During a live burn you see smoke pulsing — breathing in and out of the doorway — and hear a high-pitched whistling from a cracked window. A classmate wants to push in. What are you seeing, and what’s the order?","a":"Backdraft indicators: the pulsing means the fire is gasping at the edge of its oxygen supply, and the whistling is air being drawn through the crack. Order: do NOT open that door — cool the space first, transitional attack through an opening if possible, and control doors. His entry would be the ignition event.","why":"A ventilation-limited, superheated compartment is a bomb waiting for air. Backdraft doesn’t negotiate and doesn’t warn twice. The recruit who recognizes the signs and stops the crew just earned their place."},{"sec":"Fire behavior","q":"The instructor keeps repeating: ‘Your entry IS the ventilation event.’ In a ventilation-limited structure, explain exactly what opening the front door does to the fire — and what must be true before you open it.","a":"The open door becomes a new intake: fresh oxygen floods an oxygen-starved, superheated compartment, the heat release rate spikes, and the fire races toward the new exhaust — possibly to flashover. Before opening it, water must be ready and applied — transitional or immediate direct attack — with doors behind you controlled so you aren’t standing in the new flow path.","why":"This ties the academy skills together: fire behavior explains WHY the ventilation and attack doctrines exist. Every tactic you’ve learned is a different answer to the same fact — air is the trigger, and you’re the one carrying it in."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Academy drill: raise a 24-foot extension ladder to a second-floor window for rescue. Walk through the placement — angle, footing, securing.","a":"Butt it on firm, level ground; raise to a 75-degree climbing angle — the 4:1 rule, butt out one foot for every four feet of working length; heel it or keep it heeled, tie off the halyard, and lock the pawls before anyone climbs.","why":"Ladder fundamentals are pass/fail for a reason: wrong angle means it slides out or tips back, unlocked dogs mean it collapses under load, and an unheeled butt on slick ground is a fall waiting for both the victim and the rescuer."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"Fire showing from a second-floor window, victim reported at the adjacent bedroom window. Your partner starts raising the ladder to the fire window ‘to get water on it faster.’ Why is this wrong — and where does the ladder go?","a":"Wrong window. Ladders go to the rescue window first — the victim’s window — not the fire window. Placing at the fire exposes ladder and climbers to direct flame and heat, puts the rescue in the exhaust path, and delays the actual rescue. The fire window gets the line; the victim window gets the ladder.","why":"Academy priority order: rescue first, and the ladder is a rescue tool before it’s an access tool. Heat-weakened metal fails without warning — never climb a ladder that’s been in direct flame."},{"sec":"Ladder ops","q":"You need to work from the ladder at the roofline, pulling siding to check for extension. What’s the technique that keeps you on the ladder — and what are your free hands doing?","a":"Leg lock: climb a few rungs above your work point, pass one leg behind the rungs and hook the foot, leaning your weight into the lock — that frees both hands. With them you’re pulling siding, sounding the roof, or running a tool. Never work one-handed and unsecured at height.","why":"The leg lock is the difference between working from the ladder and falling off it. Recruits rush it; veterans do it every single time, even for ‘just a second’ — because ‘just a second’ is when gravity collects."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"First live-burn evolution: you’re on the nozzle approaching the fire room. Fog or straight stream — and where does the water go first?","a":"Straight stream for reach and penetration, applied to the seat of the fire from the unburned side — cool the overhead and the fire in a controlled pattern. Wide fog in the approach just makes steam that cooks your own crew and pushes heat; save fog patterns for hydraulic ventilation after knockdown.","why":"The burn building punishes textbook answers applied blindly. A straight stream reaches the seat through the thermal layer; fog near the nozzle in a hot hallway converts to a steam burn on your crew. Technique follows conditions, not habit."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Interior crew is at the burn-room door ready to enter. The exterior crew is still setting up their transitional line. Go or wait? What’s the coordinated answer, and why?","a":"Wait. Coordinated attack means exterior water hits first — collapsing the heat release rate — and the interior crew enters on the improved conditions, timed with ventilation. Entering early makes the interior crew the ventilation event. Thirty seconds of patience buys survivable conditions.","why":"Live fire rewards coordination and punishes heroics. The crew that waits for the water and enters together on a cooled fire looks slow and goes home. The crew that rushes looks aggressive and gets burned."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Mid-evolution: heat building fast at the ceiling, the smoke layer dropping, a low roar underneath it all. Your instinct says push a little further. What does the instructor want to hear instead?","a":"‘Backing out.’ Those are deteriorating conditions trending toward flashover — dropping neutral plane, rising heat, audible warning. Withdraw to a safe area, report conditions, and re-attack with water from a tenable position. There is no shame in backing out; there is a line-of-duty death in pushing through.","why":"The burn building is the one place you’re allowed to learn this lesson safely — the instructors are watching for exactly this decision. Firefighters who can’t back out in training won’t back out on the fireground, and that’s where it turns fatal."},{"sec":"Live fire","q":"Live-fire search evolution: charged line at the door, a partner, victim reported in the rear bedroom. How do you move — and what protects the victim from your own attack?","a":"Stay low below the thermal layer, oriented to the wall or line, moving fast and systematically — search the bedroom while the line protects the hallway and your exit. And coordinate with the nozzle: water goes on the fire, never blindly into the search area — steam and driven heat kill victims faster than the fire you were trying to beat.","why":"Live-fire search is the whole academy skill set in one evolution: SCBA discipline, fire-behavior reads, ventilation awareness, and the attack protecting the search. The victim’s survivability clock is running — but a panicked, uncoordinated search just adds bodies to the count."},{"q":"During a live-fire evolution, conditions suddenly deteriorate — extreme heat, zero visibility, disorientation. What's your immediate action?","a":"Call it out, activate PASS if separated, and withdraw along the hose line or wall to the exit — exactly like the emergency procedure brief. Don't try to push through in training.","why":"The burn building is where you practice the withdrawal, not where you prove toughness. Firefighters who can't back out in training won't back out on the fireground.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"You're assigned as the backup team at the door during a burn. What's your actual job?","a":"Full PPE, on air, charged line, watching the entry point and the clock — ready to go in the moment the interior team calls for help or misses a check-in. Backup isn't standby; it's a rescue team pre-deployed.","why":"The backup team is the evolution's insurance policy. A backup team that's not ready is just spectators in turnout gear.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"The instructor asks if your PPE and SCBA passed inspection this morning. You skipped the check because it 'was fine yesterday.' What's wrong?","a":"Everything — live fire is the worst place to discover a failed regulator, a torn liner, or a low bottle. Pre-evolution checks are mandatory because the margin in a burn building is thin.","why":"Gear failures in training are embarrassing; gear failures in a live burn are injuries. The check takes two minutes. The burn takes seconds.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"Why does the course stress that burn building fires behave differently than real structure fires?","a":"Because the fuel loads are controlled, the ventilation is designed, and instructors are managing the evolution — it's safer by design, which means the lessons about reading conditions still apply but the consequences are managed. Don't mistake training safety for fireground safety.","why":"Overconfidence from clean burns kills on real fires. The burn building teaches skills; only real fires teach respect. Keep them separate in your head.","src":"Video: Prerequisites for Live Fire Training Participants","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"Single-story home, fire in the living room, possible victim — where do you search first?","a":"Bedrooms and the paths to exits — that's where people are when fire strikes, especially at night. Sweep the bedrooms first, then bathrooms and closets, working back toward the fire with the line protecting you.","why":"The experiments show victims cluster in predictable locations. Searching randomly wastes the survivability window; searching smart buys lives.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"You're searching and find a closed bedroom door with no fire showing. Open it or skip it?","a":"Open it — carefully, staying low, ready to close it again. Closed doors protect victims; that room is the most likely survivable space in the house. Check it.","why":"A closed door is a victim's best friend. The experiments confirm survivable conditions persist behind closed doors long after the hallway becomes untenable. Never skip a closed door.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"The attack crew is hitting the fire from the exterior (transitional) while you prepare to search. How does that change your search?","a":"It makes it survivable — the exterior knockdown drops temperatures and buys time. Coordinate: confirm water's on the fire, then search fast and systematically while conditions are improved.","why":"Search without suppression is a race against flashover; search after transitional attack is a rescue in a cooled building. The nozzle is the search team's best protection.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons"},{"q":"You've searched the bedrooms and found no one, but dispatch said 'child possibly inside.' What's next?","a":"Re-search — expand to bathrooms, closets, under beds, behind furniture, and re-check rooms as conditions change. Kids hide. And communicate: tell command the primary search is complete with negative results so they can decide on a secondary.","why":"Children don't wait by the door — they hide from the scary thing. A negative primary search isn't clearance; it's the start of the secondary. Report it clearly so command has real information.","src":"Video: Search and Rescue Tactics in Single-Family Single-Story Residential Structures","sec":"Video lessons"}],"diagrams":[]}];

